# Server components

This matters in Next.js's app router and anywhere else with React Server Components. In an app that's all client, such as one built with Vite alone, none of it applies.

## The components carry the directive

A part that holds state, uses an effect or listens for events has `"use client"` at the top of its own file in the package, and the package's build checks that it's still there. So a server component can render it:

```tsx
// app/page.tsx: a server component, with no directive
import { Dialog, DialogContent, DialogTitle, DialogTrigger, Button } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <Dialog>
      <DialogTrigger asChild>
        <Button>Open</Button>
      </DialogTrigger>
      <DialogContent>
        <DialogTitle>Details</DialogTitle>
      </DialogContent>
    </Dialog>
  );
}
```

Some components have no state and no directive, and render on the server as they are: `Badge`, `Button`, `Card`, `Alert`, `Input`, `Skeleton`, `Spinner`, `Stat` and others. Each reference file has a "Server components" section that says which kind its component is.

Don't add `"use client"` to a file only because it imports from `@nuvui/react`.

## When your file needs it

Put `"use client"` at the top of a file of yours when that file:

- calls a hook: `useState`, `useEffect`, a form library's, `useDataTable`;
- passes a function as a prop: `onClick`, `onValueChange`, `onOpenChange`;
- passes a function as a child, which a few components take, such as `MapClusters`;
- calls `toast()`.

A function can't cross from a server component to a client one. A server action is the exception, in Next.js: a function in a file marked `"use server"` can be imported into a client file and called from a handler there.

Otherwise it can't. That's the usual cause of the error about functions not being passable to client components: an `onValueChange` written in a server component. Move the part with the handler into a small client component, and leave the page around it on the server.

## Fetch on the server, pass down

```tsx
// app/people/page.tsx: server
export default async function Page() {
  const people = await getPeople();
  return <PeopleTable people={people} />;
}
```

```tsx
// app/people/people-table.tsx: client, because of the hook
"use client";

import { DataTable } from "@nuvui/table";
import { createColumnHelper, useDataTable } from "@nuvui/table/full";
```

What's passed down has to be plain data: no functions, no class instances. A `Date` is fine.

## The ones that are always client, in your file

- **A chart.** The file a chart is written in has `"use client"`: Recharts measures the page.
- **A data table.** `useDataTable` is a hook.
- **A map.** `MapView` renders from a server component with plain props, and draws in the browser. `setMapWorkerUrl` is called from a client file.
- **The editor.** Load it lazily, with `next/dynamic` or `lazy`: it's large.

## Stylesheets

Import the stylesheets in the root layout, which is a server component. They're plain CSS files: no loader, no plugin, and nothing that needs the client.

## What's rendered before the browser has it

Dates and times differ between the server and the reader's browser: the day, the time zone. Don't render "today" on the server. [reference/date-picker/index.md](page:date-picker/index) covers what the date components do about it and what's left to you.

# Styling

nuvui's styles are plain CSS, built from SCSS, in three cascade layers: `tokens`, `base`, `components`. Every rule of a component is on a class that begins with `nuv-`. Nothing in it is a Tailwind utility, and nothing in it needs Tailwind.

## Load the stylesheet, once

A component never imports CSS. The app does, once, where every page gets it:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx, or the file the app starts in
import "@nuvui/react/styles.css";
import "@nuvui/table/styles.css"; // each add-on that's used, after the core
```

A component that renders as bare, unstyled HTML means its stylesheet isn't loaded. Don't fix that by writing styles for it.

## Change a look in this order

1. **A prop.** `intent`, `size`, `variant`, `orientation`. The reference file's props table lists them.
2. **A component variable.** Each component reads `--nuv-<component>-*` variables, listed in its reference file under "CSS variables". Set one on the component or on anything around it.
3. **A token.** `--color-primary`, `--radius-md`, `--spacing`. Changing one changes everything that reads it. [tokens.md](tool:get_tokens) has them all.

```css
/* Wrong: fighting the component with a heavier selector */
.toolbar .nuv-button {
  border-radius: 9999px !important;
}

/* Right: the variable the button reads */
.toolbar {
  --nuv-button-radius: var(--radius-full);
}
```

You never need `!important` or a longer selector. The library's rules are in cascade layers, and a rule that's in no layer beats every rule in one, whichever file loads first. If the app's own CSS is in layers, the layer with the overrides has to come after `tokens` and `components`.

## Every color is a token

```css
/* Wrong */
.invoice__total {
  color: #16a34a;
}

/* Right */
.invoice__total {
  color: var(--color-success);
}
```

The semantic colors have a value for light and one for dark, and each pair is checked for contrast. A color written out has neither. Use the semantic ones, `--color-primary`, `--color-foreground`, `--color-muted-foreground`, `--color-border`, `--color-surface`, `--color-danger`, `--color-success`, `--color-warning`, and not the raw scales such as `--color-blue-600`, which don't change in dark mode. Text on a filled color uses its pair: `--color-primary-foreground` on `--color-primary`.

The same goes for a color in JSX: `style={{ color: "var(--color-danger)" }}`, never `"red"`.

## `className` places a component, it doesn't restyle it

Every component takes `className` and `style`, and merges yours with its own. Use them to put the component somewhere: a margin, a grid area, a width.

```tsx
// Wrong: utilities standing in for the component's own look
<Button className="rounded-full bg-green-600 px-8 text-white">Save</Button>

// Right: the look is a prop and a variable, the class is for the layout
<Button intent="primary" className="settings__save">Save</Button>
```

If the app has Tailwind, its utilities are fine for the layout around components, such as `className="mt-4 w-full"`. [reference/tailwind.md](page:tailwind) says how to set the layer order so the two don't fight, and how to get `bg-primary` and the like from the library's tokens. If the app has no Tailwind, don't add it.

## Dark mode and density are attributes

```html
<html data-theme="system" data-density="comfortable">
```

- `data-theme` is `light`, `dark` or `system`. Without it, everything is light. It can go on any element, to theme part of a page.
- `data-density` sets how tall controls are with a mouse. [reference/theming.md](page:theming) has the levels.
- There's no theme provider, no theme object and no hook. Don't write one. A switch for dark mode sets the attribute on `<html>`.

A preset is one more stylesheet, after the core's: `import "@nuvui/react/themes/ink.css"`. [reference/theming.md](page:theming) lists them.

## Your own CSS

Write it the way the app already does: its CSS modules, its SCSS, its Tailwind. Where the app has no styles of its own yet, the default is a plain CSS file beside the component, imported by it, with class names in BEM. Not inline styles, and not a new tool.

The examples in the reference files lay things out with inline `style`, in pixels. That's to keep each example in one file. Don't copy it: in an app, the layout goes in a stylesheet, in tokens.

For a screen or a block you write, follow what the library and its blocks do:

- **BEM, with your block's name.** `.invoice`, `.invoice__total`, `.invoice--overdue`. Never a class that begins with `nuv-`: those are the library's, and may change.
- **Tokens for everything that has one**: colors, `calc(var(--spacing) * 4)` for space, `var(--radius-md)`, `var(--text-sm)`.
- **Smallest screen first.** Write the phone's layout, and add to it at a breakpoint.
- **Logical properties**: `inline-size`, `margin-inline-start`, `inset-inline-end`, so the layout mirrors where text reads from the right.
- **Targets of 44 pixels** on a touch screen.

With Sass, the library's mixins do the breakpoints, the focus ring and hover that doesn't stick on touch:

```scss
@use "@nuvui/react/scss/mixins";

.invoice__row {
  display: grid;
  gap: calc(var(--spacing) * 2);

  @include mixins.breakpoint("md") {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
  }

  @include mixins.focus-ring;
}
```

[reference/scss.md](page:scss) lists them. Without Sass, write the media query and use `:focus-visible` with `outline: 2px solid var(--color-ring)`.

A CSS variable can't be read in a media query, so in plain CSS a breakpoint is written out. They're the ones in [tokens.md](tool:get_tokens): `sm` 40rem, `md` 48rem, `lg` 64rem, `xl` 80rem, `2xl` 96rem.

```css
.invoice__row {
  display: grid;
  gap: calc(var(--spacing) * 2);
}

@media (min-width: 48rem) {
  .invoice__row {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
  }
}
```

The library styles its components and little else. A page's own frame, its width, the space around it, its headings, is the app's to write.

## Under a Content Security Policy

A site that sends a Content Security Policy needs `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'` for nuvui. Components set some styles on the element, such as how far a progress bar has filled, and a page rendered on a server sends them as `style` attributes. Without that line they're dropped, and a progress bar is empty, a slider's handle sits at the start and a scroll area doesn't scroll. Keep `style-src` itself strict: the one `<style>` element in the library is `ScrollArea`'s, and it takes the policy's nonce as `nonce`. The whole policy, line by line: [reference/content-security-policy.md](page:content-security-policy).

Don't fix a blocked style by moving it into a `<style>` element, or by adding `'unsafe-inline'` to `style-src`.

## Don't

- Don't copy a component's CSS into the app to change it. Set its variables.
- Don't style a `nuv-` class that the reference doesn't list. The listed classes and variables are what's kept from one version to the next.
- Don't set a `z-index` to get an overlay on top. Dialogs, popovers and toasts are stacked by the library's own tokens.
- Don't animate without `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` around it.

sections.markdown:
## Use a component

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Page() {
  return <Button>Save changes</Button>;
}
```

## Loading less

`styles.css` contains every component. If you only use a few, import their stylesheets one by one. Each needs the tokens and the base styles loaded as well:

```tsx
import "@nuvui/react/tokens.css";
import "@nuvui/react/base.css";
import "@nuvui/react/css/button.css";
```

The JavaScript has an entry per component too. Bundlers tree-shake the main entry, so this mostly matters if yours doesn't:

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react/button";
```

