      {/* A native control: register() gives it its name, ref and handlers. */}
      <Field required>
        <FieldLabel>Full name</FieldLabel>
        <FieldControl>
          <Input
            // Someone else's name, so the browser isn't asked to offer the
            // reader's own.
            autoComplete="off"
            {...register("name", { required: "Enter their name." })}
          />
        </FieldControl>
        <FieldError>{errors.name?.message}</FieldError>
      </Field>

      <Field required>
        <FieldLabel>Email</FieldLabel>
        <FieldControl>
          <Input
            type="email"
            autoComplete="off"
            {...register("email", {
              required: "Enter their email address.",
              pattern: {
                value: /^\S+@\S+\.\S+$/,
                message: "Enter an email address, like ada@example.com.",
              },
            })}
          />
        </FieldControl>
        <FieldDescription>The invite goes to this address.</FieldDescription>
        <FieldError>{errors.email?.message}</FieldError>
      </Field>

      {/* A Radix control has no change event to register, so a Controller
          hands it the value and takes the new one back. */}
      <Field required>
        <FieldLabel>Role</FieldLabel>
        <Controller
          name="role"
          control={control}
          rules={{ required: "Pick a role." }}
          render={({ field }) => (
            <Select value={field.value} onValueChange={field.onChange}>
              <FieldControl>
                {/* The ref is what lets the form focus this on an error. */}
                <SelectTrigger
                  ref={field.ref}
                  onBlur={field.onBlur}
                  style={{ "--nuv-select-width": "100%" } as never}
                >
                  <SelectValue placeholder="Pick a role" />
                </SelectTrigger>
              </FieldControl>
              <SelectContent>
                <SelectItem value="viewer">Viewer</SelectItem>
                <SelectItem value="commenter">Commenter</SelectItem>
                <SelectItem value="editor">Editor</SelectItem>
                <SelectItem value="billing">Billing</SelectItem>
                <SelectItem value="admin">Admin</SelectItem>
                <SelectItem value="owner">Owner</SelectItem>
              </SelectContent>
            </Select>
          )}
        />
        <FieldError>{errors.role?.message}</FieldError>
      </Field>

      <Field>
        <FieldLabel>Note</FieldLabel>
        <FieldControl>
          <Textarea
            autoResize
            {...register("note", {
              maxLength: {
                value: 200,
                message: "Keep it under 200 characters.",
              },
            })}
          />
        </FieldControl>
        <FieldDescription>
          Optional. They see it in the invite.
        </FieldDescription>
        <FieldError>{errors.note?.message}</FieldError>
      </Field>

      <Field orientation="horizontal">
        <Controller
          name="terms"
          control={control}
          rules={{ required: "Confirm that they've agreed." }}
          render={({ field }) => (
            <FieldControl>
              <Checkbox
                ref={field.ref}
                checked={field.value}
                onCheckedChange={(checked) => field.onChange(checked === true)}
                onBlur={field.onBlur}
              />
            </FieldControl>
          )}
        />
        <FieldLabel>They've agreed to be contacted</FieldLabel>
        <FieldError>{errors.terms?.message}</FieldError>
      </Field>

      <Button type="submit" style={{ justifySelf: "start" }}>
        Send invite
      </Button>
      <output aria-live="polite">{sent}</output>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Four things in that example are worth pointing out.

**`noValidate` on the form.** It turns the browser's own messages off, so yours are the only ones. `required` on `Field` still marks the control as required for screen readers and puts the mark on the label.

**`register` for native controls.** Input, Textarea, NativeSelect and PasswordInput are native elements and forward their ref, so `{...register("name")}` works on them inside `FieldControl` as it would on a bare `<input>`.

**`Controller` for Radix controls.** Select, Checkbox, Switch, RadioGroup, Slider and ToggleGroup report a value through a callback, not a change event. `Controller` passes the value in and takes the new one back. Pass its `ref` on to the part that takes focus, the trigger of a select or the checkbox itself. That's what lets the form move focus there when the field fails.

**`FieldError` takes the message as it is.** `errors.name?.message` is `undefined` while the field is fine, and `FieldError` renders nothing for it. When there is a message, the control gets `aria-invalid` and is described by it, with nothing else to wire up.

## Setting it up

Put `dir="rtl"` on the `<html>` element, and `DirectionProvider` around the app with the same value.

```tsx title="app/layout.tsx"
import { DirectionProvider } from "@nuvui/react";
import type { ReactNode } from "react";

export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="ar" dir="rtl">
      <body>
        <DirectionProvider dir="rtl">{children}</DirectionProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

`DirectionProvider` is a client component that adds no element of its own. It can be rendered from a server component, as here.

In an app with several languages, work the direction out from the language once and pass it to both places.

## In your own components

`useDirection` returns the direction from the nearest provider, or `"ltr"` when there is none.

```tsx
"use client";

import { useDirection } from "@nuvui/react";

function NextArrow() {
  const dir = useDirection();
  return <span aria-hidden="true">{dir === "rtl" ? "←" : "→"}</span>;
}
```

Most of the time CSS is enough and no hook is needed:

- Use logical properties: `margin-inline-start`, `inset-inline-end`, `border-start-start-radius`, `text-align: start`.
- Mirror what can't be expressed that way with the `dir` attribute.

```css
[dir="rtl"] .back-arrow {
  transform: scaleX(-1);
}
```

In SCSS the library's [`rtl` mixin](page:scss) writes that selector for you, and leaves a part marked `dir="ltr"` inside a right-to-left page alone:

```scss
@use "@nuvui/react/scss/mixins";

.back-arrow {
  @include mixins.rtl {
    transform: scaleX(-1);
  }
}
```

The `:dir(rtl)` selector says the same in fewer words, and isn't used here for a reason. A build that supports browsers without it, which is Next.js by default, rewrites it to a list of `:lang()` selectors. After that nothing is mirrored on a page that sets `dir="rtl"` and not one of those languages.

## How to translate them

Pass the prop wherever you render the component. With many dialogs in an app, that's easiest in a component of your own that wraps the library's:

```tsx
import { DialogContent, type DialogContentProps } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useTranslations } from "your-i18n-library";

export function AppDialogContent(props: DialogContentProps) {
  const t = useTranslations();
  return <DialogContent closeLabel={t("close")} {...props} />;
}
```

There's no provider that takes all the strings at once. A prop is found by TypeScript when its name changes, works in a server component, and keeps a component's text next to the component.

Text direction is a separate matter, covered in [right-to-left](page:right-to-left).

## The policy

These are the lines nuvui needs. Your framework adds its own, mostly for its scripts.

```txt title="Content-Security-Policy"
default-src 'self';
style-src 'self' 'nonce-{nonce}';
style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline';
img-src 'self' blob:;
connect-src 'self';
worker-src 'self' blob:;
```

`{nonce}` is a value made fresh for every response. It needs a server that sends each page. A site exported as static files has no nonce to give, and a component that needs one can't be used there under this policy.

| Line | What it's for |
| --- | --- |
| `style-src 'self'` | The packages' stylesheets, served from your site. |
| `'nonce-{nonce}'` | The one `<style>` element in the library, written by `ScrollArea`. Pass it the same value: `<ScrollArea nonce={nonce}>`. No other component writes a `<style>` element. |
| `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'` | `style` attributes in the HTML. See below. |
| `img-src blob:` | A picture of a file before it's sent, in `PromptInput`, and saving a barcode or a QR code as a PNG. Leave it out if you use neither. |
| `worker-src blob:` | Where MapLibre starts its worker from, for `@nuvui/map`. With `setMapWorkerUrl` pointing at a file on your site, `'self'` is enough. |

No package runs code from a string, so `script-src` needs no `'unsafe-eval'`. The chat package's code colors come from Shiki's JavaScript engine, which needs no WebAssembly either.

## Import

```tsx
import {
  Accordion,
  AccordionContent,
  AccordionItem,
  AccordionTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
```

The styles come from `@nuvui/react/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app. [Getting started](page:index) covers that, and how to load only this component's CSS.

## Several open at once

With `type="multiple"`, opening an item leaves the others as they are.

```tsx
import {
  Accordion,
  AccordionContent,
  AccordionItem,
  AccordionTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Accordion
      type="multiple"
      defaultValue={["size", "color"]}
      style={{ inlineSize: "100%" }}
    >
      <AccordionItem value="size">
        <AccordionTrigger>Size</AccordionTrigger>
        <AccordionContent>Small, medium and large.</AccordionContent>
      </AccordionItem>
      <AccordionItem value="color">
        <AccordionTrigger>Color</AccordionTrigger>
        <AccordionContent>Black, gray and blue.</AccordionContent>
      </AccordionItem>
      <AccordionItem value="material">
        <AccordionTrigger>Material</AccordionTrigger>
        <AccordionContent>Cotton and linen.</AccordionContent>
      </AccordionItem>
    </Accordion>
  );
}
```

## Where the bar is

`position` has three values.

- `"fixed"`, the default, puts the bar along the bottom of the window, over the page. Leave room at the end of the page so the bar doesn't cover the last row.
- `"sticky"` keeps it at the bottom of whatever scrolls around it, and it takes room there.
- `"static"` leaves it where you put it.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  ActionBar,
  ActionBarSelection,
  Button,
  Checkbox,
  Label,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

const files = Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, index) => ({
  id: `file-${index + 1}`,
  name: `Report ${index + 1}.pdf`,
}));

export default function Example() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string[]>(["file-2", "file-3"]);
  const toggle = (id: string) =>
    setSelected((current) =>
      current.includes(id)
        ? current.filter((other) => other !== id)
        : [...current, id],
    );

  return (
    <section
      aria-label="Files"
      // biome-ignore lint/a11y/noNoninteractiveTabindex: a box that scrolls has to be reachable by keyboard
      tabIndex={0}
      style={{
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 360,
        blockSize: 260,
        overflow: "auto",
        paddingInline: 12,
        border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
        borderRadius: 8,
      }}
    >
      <ul
        style={{
          display: "grid",
          gap: 8,
          margin: 0,
          paddingBlock: 12,
          paddingInline: 0,
          listStyle: "none",
        }}
      >
        {files.map((file) => (
          <li
            key={file.id}
            style={{ display: "flex", gap: 8, alignItems: "center" }}
          >
            <Checkbox
              id={`action-bar-${file.id}`}
              checked={selected.includes(file.id)}
              onCheckedChange={() => toggle(file.id)}
            />
            <Label htmlFor={`action-bar-${file.id}`}>{file.name}</Label>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <ActionBar
        open={selected.length > 0}
        position="sticky"
        aria-label="Selected files"
      >
        <ActionBarSelection>{selected.length} selected</ActionBarSelection>
        <Button intent="secondary" size="sm" onClick={() => setSelected([])}>
          Download
        </Button>
        <Button intent="danger" size="sm" onClick={() => setSelected([])}>
          Delete
        </Button>
      </ActionBar>
    </section>
  );
}
```

A closed bar takes no room in any of the three.

## When a screen reader reads it

An alert has a `role` that depends on its intent.

| Intent | Role | What a screen reader does when the alert is added to the page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `info`, `success` | `status` | Reads it at the next pause. |
| `warning`, `danger` | `alert` | Stops what it's saying and reads it at once. |

That only happens for an alert that's added after the page has loaded, as in the example below. One that's in the page from the start isn't read out. It's read when the visitor gets to it, like any other text.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Alert, AlertDescription, AlertTitle, Button } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: 12,
        justifyItems: "start",
        width: "100%",
        maxWidth: 480,
      }}
    >
      <Button onClick={() => setFailed((value) => !value)}>
        {failed ? "Clear the error" : "Save with an error"}
      </Button>
      {failed ? (
        <Alert intent="danger" style={{ width: "100%" }}>
          <AlertTitle>The changes weren't saved</AlertTitle>
          <AlertDescription>
            The server didn't answer. Try again in a moment.
          </AlertDescription>
        </Alert>
      ) : null}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Two cases call for a different role:

- **A warning that's part of the page**, such as a note above a form about what the form does. It was never news, so `role="note"` describes it better. Nothing changes for an alert that's there from the start, but the role is also what a screen reader says when the visitor reaches it.
- **Many alerts added at once.** Each one with `role="alert"` interrupts the one before. Put the messages in one alert.

A screen reader only notices an alert that's added to the page. One that's there all the time and shown with CSS, or whose text you change, is announced in some combinations of browser and screen reader and not in others. Render the alert when there's something to say, as the example does.

pages.markdown:
# Alert dialog

A dialog that stops everything to ask one question, with a button to go ahead and a button to back out.

Use an alert dialog to confirm something that can't be taken back: deleting a project, revoking access, leaving a form with unsaved changes. It differs from a [dialog](page:components/dialog) in three ways. A click outside doesn't close it, it has no close button in its corner, and focus starts on the button that changes nothing.

```tsx
import {
  AlertDialog,
  AlertDialogAction,
  AlertDialogCancel,
  AlertDialogContent,
  AlertDialogDescription,
  AlertDialogFooter,
  AlertDialogTitle,
  AlertDialogTrigger,
  Button,
} from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <AlertDialog>
      <AlertDialogTrigger asChild>
        <Button intent="danger">Delete project</Button>
      </AlertDialogTrigger>
      <AlertDialogContent>
        <AlertDialogTitle>Delete this project?</AlertDialogTitle>
        <AlertDialogDescription>
          The project and its 12 files will be removed. This can't be undone.
        </AlertDialogDescription>
        <AlertDialogFooter>
          <AlertDialogCancel>Cancel</AlertDialogCancel>
          <AlertDialogAction intent="danger">Delete project</AlertDialogAction>
        </AlertDialogFooter>
      </AlertDialogContent>
    </AlertDialog>
  );
}
```

## Install

```sh
pnpm add @nuvui/react
```

## Import

```tsx
import {
  AlertDialog,
  AlertDialogAction,
  AlertDialogCancel,
  AlertDialogContent,
  AlertDialogDescription,
  AlertDialogFooter,
  AlertDialogTitle,
  AlertDialogTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
```

The styles come from `@nuvui/react/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app. [Getting started](page:index) covers that. If you load one stylesheet per component, this one needs `button.css` next to `alert-dialog.css`, because its two buttons are the library's [Button](page:components/button).

## Parts

| Part | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `AlertDialog` | Holds the open state. Renders nothing itself. |
| `AlertDialogTrigger` | The button that opens the dialog. Wrap your own with `asChild`. |
| `AlertDialogContent` | The panel. It also renders the overlay behind it. |
| `AlertDialogTitle` | The question. Required, because it's the dialog's accessible name. |
| `AlertDialogDescription` | What will happen. It becomes the dialog's accessible description. |
| `AlertDialogFooter` | A row for the two buttons. |
| `AlertDialogCancel` | The button that backs out. It closes the dialog, and is where focus starts. |
| `AlertDialogAction` | The button that goes ahead. It closes the dialog after its `onClick` has run. |

These are separate exports, not properties of `AlertDialog`. `AlertDialog.Content` isn't available, because property access on a client component doesn't work from a server component.

## Props

### AlertDialogContent

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `size` | `"lg" \| "md" \| "sm"` | `"md"` | The widest the dialog gets once the screen is big enough to center it. On a phone it's always a full-width sheet. |
| `container` | `DocumentFragment \| Element \| null` | `document.body` | The element the dialog is rendered into. Set this when the dialog should pick up a `data-theme` from a section of the page. |

### AlertDialogAction and AlertDialogCancel

Both are a [Button](page:components/button) and take every prop a button does. `AlertDialogCancel` starts with `intent="secondary"`, and `AlertDialogAction` with the button's own default, `"primary"`.

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `intent` | `"danger" \| "ghost" \| "primary" \| "secondary"` | `"primary"` | How the button looks. Use `"danger"` on the action when it destroys something. |
| `onClick` | `MouseEventHandler<HTMLButtonElement>` |  | Runs before the dialog closes. Call `event.preventDefault()` in it to keep the dialog open. |
| `disabled` | `boolean` |  | Stops the button from being pressed. |
| `asChild` | `boolean` | `false` | Renders the element you pass in place of a `button`, such as a link, with the button's look and the closing behavior. |

### AlertDialog

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `open` | `boolean` |  | Whether it's open, when you control it yourself. Use it with `onOpenChange`. |
| `defaultOpen` | `boolean` |  | Whether it starts open, when you don't control it. |
| `onOpenChange` | `((open: boolean) => void)` |  | Called when it opens or closes. |

`AlertDialogTrigger`, `AlertDialogTitle` and `AlertDialogDescription` take the same props as the Radix parts they wrap. The [Radix Alert Dialog reference](https://www.radix-ui.com/primitives/docs/components/alert-dialog#api-reference) lists them. `AlertDialogFooter` is a plain `div`.

## Waiting for the action to finish

By default the action closes the dialog at once. When the work can fail, or takes a moment, keep the dialog open until it's done: control `open`, and call `event.preventDefault()` in the action's `onClick`.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  AlertDialog,
  AlertDialogAction,
  AlertDialogCancel,
  AlertDialogContent,
  AlertDialogDescription,
  AlertDialogFooter,
  AlertDialogTitle,
  AlertDialogTrigger,
  Button,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { type MouseEvent, useRef, useState } from "react";

// Stands in for a request to your server. So that both endings can be
// seen, the first one fails and the next goes through.
let tried = 0;
async function revokeAccess() {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 600));
  tried += 1;
  if (tried === 1) throw new Error("The server didn't answer.");
}

export default function Example() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [revoking, setRevoking] = useState(false);
  const [revoked, setRevoked] = useState(false);
  const [problem, setProblem] = useState("");
  const status = useRef<HTMLParagraphElement>(null);

  async function revoke(event: MouseEvent) {
    // Keeps the dialog open. It closes below, once the request is through.
    event.preventDefault();
    setRevoking(true);
    setProblem("");
    try {
      await revokeAccess();
      setRevoked(true);
      setOpen(false);
    } catch {
      // It didn't go through, so the dialog stays, says so, and can be
      // tried again or left.
      setProblem("Access couldn't be revoked. Try again.");
    } finally {
      setRevoking(false);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "center" }}>
      <AlertDialog
        open={open}
        onOpenChange={(next) => {
          setOpen(next);
          if (!next) setProblem("");
        }}
      >
        <AlertDialogTrigger asChild>
          <Button intent="secondary" disabled={revoked}>
            Revoke access
          </Button>
        </AlertDialogTrigger>
        <AlertDialogContent
          size="sm"
          onCloseAutoFocus={(event) => {
            // Once it's done, the button that opened this is disabled and
            // can't take the focus back. The line that says what happened
            // takes it.
            if (!revoked) return;
            event.preventDefault();
            status.current?.focus();
          }}
        >
          <AlertDialogTitle>Revoke Sam's access?</AlertDialogTitle>
          <AlertDialogDescription>
            Sam will be signed out and won't be able to open the workspace.
          </AlertDialogDescription>
          {problem && (
            <p role="alert" style={{ margin: 0, color: "var(--color-danger)" }}>
              {problem}
            </p>
          )}
          <AlertDialogFooter>
            <AlertDialogCancel disabled={revoking}>
              Keep access
            </AlertDialogCancel>
            <AlertDialogAction
              intent="danger"
              disabled={revoking}
              onClick={revoke}
            >
              {revoking ? "Revoking" : "Revoke access"}
            </AlertDialogAction>
          </AlertDialogFooter>
        </AlertDialogContent>
      </AlertDialog>
      <p ref={status} tabIndex={-1} style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: 14 }}>
        {revoked ? "Sam no longer has access." : "Sam has access."}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Disable both buttons while the request is running, so it can't be sent twice or abandoned halfway.

When the work fails, leave the dialog open and say so inside it, in an element with `role="alert"` so it's read out. The buttons come back, to try again or to leave. The example's first try fails, so that this can be seen.

When it's done, the button that opened the dialog is often gone or disabled, as here, and the focus has nowhere to go back to. Say where it goes in `onCloseAutoFocus`: the line that says what happened, or whatever comes after what was removed.

## Opening one from a menu

A destructive item in a [dropdown menu](page:components/dropdown-menu) should ask before it acts. Put the alert dialog next to the menu, not inside the item, and open it from the item's `onSelect`.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  AlertDialog,
  AlertDialogAction,
  AlertDialogCancel,
  AlertDialogContent,
  AlertDialogDescription,
  AlertDialogFooter,
  AlertDialogTitle,
  Button,
  DropdownMenu,
  DropdownMenuContent,
  DropdownMenuItem,
  DropdownMenuSeparator,
  DropdownMenuTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [confirming, setConfirming] = useState(false);
  const [deleted, setDeleted] = useState(false);
  const menuButton = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "center" }}>
      <DropdownMenu>
        <DropdownMenuTrigger asChild>
          <Button ref={menuButton} intent="secondary" disabled={deleted}>
            File
          </Button>
        </DropdownMenuTrigger>
        <DropdownMenuContent>
          <DropdownMenuItem>Rename</DropdownMenuItem>
          <DropdownMenuSeparator />
          <DropdownMenuItem
            intent="danger"
            onSelect={() => setConfirming(true)}
          >
            Delete
          </DropdownMenuItem>
        </DropdownMenuContent>
      </DropdownMenu>

      {/* Next to the menu, not inside the item. The menu takes its items off
          the page when it closes, and the dialog would go with them. */}
      <AlertDialog open={confirming} onOpenChange={setConfirming}>
        <AlertDialogContent
          size="sm"
          // The dialog would put focus back where it was when it opened,
          // which is the menu item, and that's gone. Send it to the button.
          onCloseAutoFocus={(event) => {
            event.preventDefault();
            menuButton.current?.focus();
          }}
        >
          <AlertDialogTitle>Delete report.pdf?</AlertDialogTitle>
          <AlertDialogDescription>
            It goes to the trash, and is removed for good after 30 days.
          </AlertDialogDescription>
          <AlertDialogFooter>
            <AlertDialogCancel>Cancel</AlertDialogCancel>
            <AlertDialogAction intent="danger" onClick={() => setDeleted(true)}>
              Delete
            </AlertDialogAction>
          </AlertDialogFooter>
        </AlertDialogContent>
      </AlertDialog>

      <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: 14 }}>
        {deleted ? "report.pdf is in the trash." : "report.pdf"}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

There's no trigger here for focus to go back to when the dialog closes. Left alone, Radix returns it to whatever had it when the dialog opened, which is the menu item, and that's no longer on the page. So the example says where it should go, in `onCloseAutoFocus`: the button that opened the menu.

## Sizes

`size` sets the maximum width once the screen is at least 40rem wide: 24rem for `sm`, 32rem for `md` and 48rem for `lg`. Below that the dialog is a sheet across the bottom of the screen, with the action on top and cancel under it, each as wide as the sheet.

## Accessibility

### Keyboard

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> | On the trigger, opens the dialog. On a button inside, presses it. |
| <kbd>Tab</kbd> | Moves between the two buttons. From the last it goes back to the first. |
| <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd> | The same, backwards. |
| <kbd>Esc</kbd> | Closes the dialog, as cancel does. |

### What the component handles

Most of this is Radix. The library doesn't change it.

- The panel has `role="alertdialog"`, named by `AlertDialogTitle` and described by `AlertDialogDescription`.
- Opening moves focus to `AlertDialogCancel`, so that pressing <kbd>Enter</kbd> without reading does no harm. Closing returns focus to the trigger.
- Focus can't leave the dialog while it's open, and the rest of the page is hidden from screen readers.
- A click or tap on the page behind does nothing. The person has to pick one of the two buttons, or press <kbd>Esc</kbd>.
- Both buttons are at least 44 pixels tall on a touch screen.
- The open and close animations only run when the visitor hasn't asked for reduced motion.

### What you need to handle

- Write the title as the question, and name the action after what it does: "Delete project", not "OK" or "Yes".
- Always include an `AlertDialogCancel`. Without one, focus has nowhere safe to start.
- Always include an `AlertDialogDescription` that says what will happen. If the title says it all, pass `aria-describedby={undefined}` to `AlertDialogContent`, or Radix logs a warning.
- If <kbd>Esc</kbd> must not close the dialog, call `event.preventDefault()` in `onEscapeKeyDown` on `AlertDialogContent`. Then the two buttons are the only ways out, so make sure both work.

## CSS variables

Alert dialogs render at the end of `<body>`, so set these on `:root` or `body`, not on the element around the trigger. They're separate from the dialog's variables: changing `--nuv-dialog-radius` doesn't change an alert dialog.

```css
:root {
  --nuv-alert-dialog-width: 28rem;
}
```

| Variable | Default | What it sets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-overlay-bg` | `var(--color-overlay)` | Color of the layer that dims the page. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-padding` | `calc(var(--spacing) * 6)` | Padding inside the panel. One length, used on every side. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-border` | `var(--color-border)` | Border color of the panel. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-bg` | `var(--color-surface)` | Background color of the panel. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-shadow` | `var(--shadow-xl)` | Shadow under the panel. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-fg` | `var(--color-surface-foreground)` | Text color. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-radius` | `var(--radius-xl)` | Corner radius. As a sheet on phones, only the top corners are rounded. |
| `--nuv-alert-dialog-width` | `32rem`, `24rem`, `48rem` | Maximum width once the dialog is centered. Differs by size. |

The two buttons read the [button's variables](page:components/button#css-variables).

## Classes

| Class | What it's on |
| --- | --- |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog` | The panel. |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog--lg` | The panel with size="lg". |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog--sm` | The panel with size="sm". |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog__description` | AlertDialogDescription. |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog__footer` | AlertDialogFooter. |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog__overlay` | The layer behind the panel. |
| `.nuv-alert-dialog__title` | AlertDialogTitle. |

The panel also gets `nuv-alert-dialog--md` at the default size. No styles are attached to it, since medium is the base. The two buttons have the button's classes, such as `nuv-button` and `nuv-button--danger`.

Radix sets `data-state="open"` or `data-state="closed"` on the overlay and the panel. The animations hang off those.

## Server components

The alert dialog parts are client components. They carry `"use client"`, and the build checks that the directive is still there in the published files.

You can put one together inside a server component, as the first example on this page does. It needs a client component of your own once the action has an `onClick`, which is most of the time.

## Themed sections

An alert dialog opened from inside a `data-theme="dark"` section doesn't inherit that theme, because it isn't rendered inside the section. Pass the section's element as `container` and it will be.

## Do and don't

**Do**

- Keep it for actions that can't be undone, or that cost something to undo.
- Say what will be lost, in numbers when you have them: "its 12 files".
- Give the action `intent="danger"` when it destroys something.

**Don't**

- Don't use one for an action that can be undone. Do it, and offer the undo in a [toast](page:components/toast).
- Don't use one to report an error or a success. Nothing is being asked.
- Don't put a form in one. That's a [dialog](page:components/dialog).

