## A group

`AvatarGroup` overlaps its avatars and draws a ring in the page's color around each, which reads as a gap between them.

```tsx
import { Avatar, AvatarFallback, AvatarGroup } from "@nuvui/react";

const people = [
  ["AL", "Ada Lovelace"],
  ["GH", "Grace Hopper"],
  ["KJ", "Katherine Johnson"],
] as const;

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <AvatarGroup role="group" aria-label="Assigned to">
      {people.map(([initials, name]) => (
        <Avatar key={initials} role="img" aria-label={name}>
          <AvatarFallback aria-hidden="true">{initials}</AvatarFallback>
        </Avatar>
      ))}
      <Avatar role="img" aria-label="and 4 more">
        <AvatarFallback aria-hidden="true">+4</AvatarFallback>
      </Avatar>
    </AvatarGroup>
  );
}
```

On a surface that isn't the page's background, such as a card, set `--nuv-avatar-group-ring` to that surface's color.

## Intents

`intent` says how much weight the action carries. A view should have one `primary` button at most.

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <Button intent="primary">Save</Button>
      <Button intent="secondary">Cancel</Button>
      <Button intent="ghost">Skip for now</Button>
      <Button intent="danger">Delete</Button>
    </>
  );
}
```

## Sizes

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <Button size="sm">Small</Button>
      <Button size="md">Medium</Button>
      <Button size="lg">Large</Button>
    </>
  );
}
```

On a touch screen every size is at least 44 pixels tall and wide, and `lg` is 48. The smaller heights (32, 40 and 48 pixels) only apply when a mouse or trackpad is the main input, and [density](page:theming#density) moves them. Sizes still differ in padding and font size on touch.

## As a link

`asChild` renders the element you pass in and gives it the button's classes. Use it for navigation, so the result is still a real link that people can open in a new tab.

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";
import Link from "next/link";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Button asChild intent="secondary">
      <Link href="/">Read the docs</Link>
    </Button>
  );
}
```

## Disabled

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <Button disabled>Save changes</Button>
      <Button intent="secondary" disabled>
        Cancel
      </Button>
    </>
  );
}
```

A disabled button can't be focused, so a keyboard user can't find out why it's disabled. If the reason isn't obvious from the page, leave the button enabled and explain what's missing when it's pressed.

## With an input

```tsx
import { Button, ButtonGroup, Input } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <ButtonGroup style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 360 }}>
      <Input type="email" aria-label="Email" placeholder="you@example.com" />
      <Button>Subscribe</Button>
    </ButtonGroup>
  );
}
```

The input takes the room the buttons leave. To put something inside the input's box instead, such as an icon or a unit, that's an [InputGroup](page:components/input-group).

## Vertical

```tsx
import { Button, ButtonGroup } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <ButtonGroup orientation="vertical" aria-label="Zoom">
      <Button intent="secondary" aria-label="Zoom in">
        +
      </Button>
      <Button intent="secondary" aria-label="Zoom out">
        −
      </Button>
    </ButtonGroup>
  );
}
```

## CSS variables

The group reads one variable of its own. Everything else about how the buttons look comes from the [button's variables](page:components/button#css-variables), which you can set on the group to change every button in it.

```css
.editor-toolbar {
  --nuv-button-radius: var(--radius-full);
}
```

| Variable | Default | What it sets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--nuv-button-group-divider` | `color-mix(in oklab, currentcolor 35%, transparent)` | Color of the line between two filled buttons, those with the primary or danger intent. |

## Content that reaches the edges

The card has no padding at its sides. Each part has it instead. So anything you put straight inside `Card`, outside the parts, runs from edge to edge: a picture, a table, a list with dividers.

A picture at the top also needs the card's top padding gone and its corners clipped.

```tsx
import {
  AspectRatio,
  Card,
  CardDescription,
  CardHeader,
  CardTitle,
} from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Card
      style={{
        width: "100%",
        maxWidth: 320,
        paddingBlockStart: 0,
        overflow: "hidden",
      }}
    >
      <AspectRatio ratio={16 / 9}>
        <div
          style={{
            background:
              "linear-gradient(135deg, var(--color-primary), var(--color-success))",
          }}
        />
      </AspectRatio>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle asChild>
          <h4>Quarterly report</h4>
        </CardTitle>
        <CardDescription>Updated on 4 July</CardDescription>
      </CardHeader>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

## More than one slide at a time

A slide is as wide as the carousel unless you say otherwise. `--nuv-carousel-slide-size` makes it a share of that, and `--nuv-carousel-gap` is the space between two. A size that leaves part of the next slide showing says there is a next slide.

```tsx
import {
  Carousel,
  CarouselContent,
  CarouselItem,
  CarouselNext,
  CarouselPrevious,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import type { CSSProperties } from "react";

const plans = ["Starter", "Team", "Business", "Enterprise", "Education"];

const slide = {
  display: "grid",
  placeItems: "center",
  blockSize: 120,
  border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
  borderRadius: "var(--radius-lg)",
  background: "var(--color-muted)",
};

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Carousel
      aria-label="Plans"
      options={{ align: "start" }}
      style={
        {
          inlineSize: "100%",
          maxInlineSize: 520,
          // Two and a bit, so the next one shows there is a next one.
          "--nuv-carousel-slide-size": "40%",
        } as CSSProperties
      }
    >
      <CarouselContent>
        {plans.map((plan) => (
          <CarouselItem key={plan}>
            <div style={slide}>{plan}</div>
          </CarouselItem>
        ))}
      </CarouselContent>
      <div style={{ display: "flex", gap: 8, justifyContent: "flex-end" }}>
        <CarouselPrevious />
        <CarouselNext />
      </div>
    </Carousel>
  );
}
```

`options` goes to the engine. `align: "start"` lines slides up with the start of the carousel, and `slidesToScroll` moves several at once.

With several slides in view there are fewer stops than slides: the carousel stops where the last slide reaches the end. `CarouselDots` draws a dot for each stop.

## Up and down

`orientation="vertical"` moves the slides up and down. Give `CarouselContent` a height: the slides fill it.

```tsx
import {
  Carousel,
  CarouselContent,
  CarouselItem,
  CarouselNext,
  CarouselPrevious,
} from "@nuvui/react";

const notes = ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday"];

const slide = {
  display: "grid",
  placeItems: "center",
  blockSize: "100%",
  border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
  borderRadius: "var(--radius-lg)",
  background: "var(--color-muted)",
};

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Carousel
      aria-label="This week"
      orientation="vertical"
      style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 320 }}
    >
      <CarouselContent style={{ blockSize: 180 }}>
        {notes.map((day) => (
          <CarouselItem key={day}>
            <div style={slide}>{day}</div>
          </CarouselItem>
        ))}
      </CarouselContent>
      <div style={{ display: "flex", gap: 8, justifyContent: "center" }}>
        <CarouselPrevious />
        <CarouselNext />
      </div>
    </Carousel>
  );
}
```

## Thumbnails

A `CarouselThumbnail` is a button that goes to one slide, with whatever you put in it. Name each one, with `aria-label` or with the `alt` of a picture inside it.

```tsx
import {
  Carousel,
  CarouselContent,
  CarouselItem,
  CarouselThumbnail,
  CarouselThumbnails,
} from "@nuvui/react";

// Stand-ins for photographs: a color and a name.
const photos = [
  { name: "Front", tone: "var(--color-blue-200)" },
  { name: "Side", tone: "var(--color-green-200)" },
  { name: "Back", tone: "var(--color-amber-200)" },
  { name: "Detail", tone: "var(--color-red-200)" },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Carousel
      aria-label="Photographs of the chair"
      style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 420 }}
    >
      <CarouselContent>
        {photos.map((photo) => (
          <CarouselItem key={photo.name}>
            <div
              style={{
                display: "grid",
                placeItems: "center",
                blockSize: 200,
                borderRadius: "var(--radius-lg)",
                background: photo.tone,
                color: "var(--color-black)",
              }}
            >
              {photo.name}
            </div>
          </CarouselItem>
        ))}
      </CarouselContent>
      <CarouselThumbnails>
        {photos.map((photo, index) => (
          <CarouselThumbnail
            key={photo.name}
            index={index}
            aria-label={photo.name}
            style={{ background: photo.tone }}
          />
        ))}
      </CarouselThumbnails>
    </Carousel>
  );
}
```

## Moving by itself

`autoplay` moves to the next slide every so many milliseconds. Think twice. Something that moves while it's being read is hard on anyone who reads slowly, and most people have left before the second slide.

With `autoplay`, put a `CarouselPlayPause` inside, before the slides, so the keyboard reaches it first. The carousel also:

- waits while the pointer is over it, and goes on when the pointer leaves
- stops when the keyboard reaches it, and stays stopped until the button starts it again
- doesn't move while its tab is in the background
- doesn't start at all for someone who has asked their system for less motion

```tsx
import {
  Carousel,
  CarouselContent,
  CarouselDots,
  CarouselItem,
  CarouselNext,
  CarouselPlayPause,
  CarouselPrevious,
} from "@nuvui/react";

const news = [
  "Invoices can be exported as CSV",
  "Roles now have a read-only kind",
  "Single sign-on is out of beta",
];

const slide = {
  display: "grid",
  placeItems: "center",
  blockSize: 140,
  padding: 16,
  border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
  borderRadius: "var(--radius-lg)",
  background: "var(--color-muted)",
  textAlign: "center",
} as const;

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Carousel
      aria-label="What is new"
      autoplay={4000}
      loop
      style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 420 }}
    >
      {/* First, so it is the first thing the keyboard reaches. */}
      <div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center", gap: 8 }}>
        <CarouselPlayPause />
        <CarouselPrevious />
        <CarouselNext />
        <CarouselDots style={{ marginInlineStart: "auto" }} />
      </div>
      <CarouselContent>
        {news.map((item) => (
          <CarouselItem key={item}>
            <div style={slide}>{item}</div>
          </CarouselItem>
        ))}
      </CarouselContent>
    </Carousel>
  );
}
```

Give each slide at least five seconds, and more for more words.

## Indeterminate

A click only ever checks or unchecks the box. The third state is one you set, with `checked="indeterminate"`, usually on a checkbox that stands for a group where some items are selected and some aren't.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Checkbox } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

const files = ["invoice.pdf", "notes.txt", "photo.jpg"];
const row = { display: "flex", alignItems: "center", gap: 8 };

export default function Example() {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string[]>(["notes.txt"]);

  const all = selected.length === files.length;
  const some = selected.length > 0 && !all;

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 20 }}>
      <div style={row}>
        <Checkbox
          id="files-all"
          checked={some ? "indeterminate" : all}
          onCheckedChange={(checked) => setSelected(checked ? files : [])}
        />
        <label htmlFor="files-all">Select all</label>
      </div>
      {files.map((file) => (
        <div key={file} style={{ ...row, paddingInlineStart: 28 }}>
          <Checkbox
            id={`file-${file}`}
            checked={selected.includes(file)}
            onCheckedChange={(checked) =>
              setSelected(
                checked
                  ? [...selected, file]
                  : selected.filter((name) => name !== file),
              )
            }
          />
          <label htmlFor={`file-${file}`}>{file}</label>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Any number

A `CheckboxCard` is a checkbox with a card around it, and each one stands alone. Group related ones in a `fieldset` with a `legend`, which is what names them together.

```tsx
import {
  CheckboxCard,
  ChoiceCardDescription,
  ChoiceCardTitle,
} from "@nuvui/react";

const addOns = [
  {
    name: "sso",
    title: "Single sign-on",
    text: "Sign in through your identity provider.",
    on: true,
  },
  {
    name: "audit",
    title: "Audit log",
    text: "Who changed what, kept for a year.",
    on: false,
  },
  {
    name: "support",
    title: "Priority support",
    text: "An answer within four working hours.",
    on: false,
    disabled: true,
  },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <fieldset
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: 8,
        gridTemplateColumns: "repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(12rem, 100%), 1fr))",
        inlineSize: "100%",
        margin: 0,
        padding: 0,
        border: 0,
      }}
    >
      <legend style={{ marginBottom: 8, padding: 0 }}>Add-ons</legend>
      {addOns.map((addOn) => (
        <CheckboxCard
          key={addOn.name}
          name={addOn.name}
          defaultChecked={addOn.on}
          disabled={addOn.disabled}
          indicator="end"
        >
          <ChoiceCardTitle>{addOn.title}</ChoiceCardTitle>
          <ChoiceCardDescription>{addOn.text}</ChoiceCardDescription>
        </CheckboxCard>
      ))}
    </fieldset>
  );
}
```

Here `indicator="end"` puts the checkbox at the far end of the card.

## Import

```tsx
import {
  Collapsible,
  CollapsibleContent,
  CollapsibleTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
```

The styles come from `@nuvui/react/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app. [Getting started](page:index) covers that, and how to load only this component's CSS.

## Showing more of a list

The trigger doesn't have to come first. Here it sits under the content, and its text says what a press will do.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Button,
  Collapsible,
  CollapsibleContent,
  CollapsibleTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

const more = ["Billing address", "Tax number", "Purchase order"];

export default function Example() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <Collapsible
      open={open}
      onOpenChange={setOpen}
      style={{ width: "100%", maxWidth: 360, fontSize: 14 }}
    >
      <ul style={{ margin: 0, paddingInlineStart: 20 }}>
        <li>Company name</li>
        <li>Contact email</li>
      </ul>
      <CollapsibleContent>
        <ul style={{ margin: 0, paddingInlineStart: 20 }}>
          {more.map((field) => (
            <li key={field}>{field}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </CollapsibleContent>
      <CollapsibleTrigger asChild>
        <Button intent="ghost" size="sm" style={{ marginBlockStart: 8 }}>
          {open ? "Show fewer fields" : `Show ${more.length} more fields`}
        </Button>
      </CollapsibleTrigger>
    </Collapsible>
  );
}
```

## In the page

Without a trigger and a popover, `ColorPickerPanel` puts the picker where you write it.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  ColorPicker,
  ColorPickerArea,
  ColorPickerHue,
  ColorPickerInput,
  ColorPickerPanel,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [color, setColor] = useState("#0d9488");

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "start" }}>
      <ColorPicker value={color} onValueChange={setColor}>
        <ColorPickerPanel>
          <ColorPickerArea />
          <ColorPickerHue />
          <ColorPickerInput />
        </ColorPickerPanel>
      </ColorPicker>
      <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: 14 }}>The color is {color}.</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Presets only

Where the colors to choose from are a list you've decided, leave out the area, the slider and the field. Give each swatch a name. Here the trigger shows the name in place of the hex.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  ColorPicker,
  ColorPickerContent,
  ColorPickerSwatch,
  ColorPickerSwatches,
  ColorPickerTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

// The colors a label can have, and no others.
const labels = [
  { value: "#b91c1c", name: "Urgent" },
  { value: "#c2410c", name: "Waiting" },
  { value: "#15803d", name: "Done" },
  { value: "#1d4ed8", name: "In review" },
  { value: "#6d28d9", name: "Idea" },
  { value: "#475569", name: "Archived" },
];

export default function Example() {
  const [color, setColor] = useState("#15803d");
  const name = labels.find((label) => label.value === color)?.name;

  return (
    <ColorPicker value={color} onValueChange={setColor}>
      <ColorPickerTrigger aria-label="Label color">{name}</ColorPickerTrigger>
      <ColorPickerContent>
        <ColorPickerSwatches aria-label="Label colors">
          {labels.map((label) => (
            <ColorPickerSwatch
              key={label.value}
              value={label.value}
              aria-label={label.name}
            />
          ))}
        </ColorPickerSwatches>
      </ColorPickerContent>
    </ColorPicker>
  );
}
```

## Saving the color

`onValueChange` is called all through a drag, many times a second. That's right for a preview. To save, use `onValueCommit`, which is called once the color has settled: when a drag ends, a key is pressed, a preset is chosen, or a typed color is entered.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  ColorPicker,
  ColorPickerArea,
  ColorPickerHue,
  ColorPickerPanel,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [color, setColor] = useState("#db2777");
  const [saved, setSaved] = useState<string[]>([]);

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "start" }}>
      <ColorPicker
        value={color}
        onValueChange={setColor}
        // Once for each drag, where onValueChange is called all through it.
        onValueCommit={(value) => setSaved((before) => [...before, value])}
      >
        <ColorPickerPanel>
          <ColorPickerArea />
          <ColorPickerHue />
        </ColorPickerPanel>
      </ColorPicker>
      <p role="status" style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: 14 }}>
        Saved {saved.length} {saved.length === 1 ? "time" : "times"}
        {saved.length > 0 ? `, last as ${saved.at(-1)}` : ""}.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## In a form

`name` puts the color in a form under that name. To give the trigger a label and a description, wrap it in a [field](page:components/field)'s `FieldControl`.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Button,
  ColorPicker,
  ColorPickerArea,
  ColorPickerContent,
  ColorPickerHue,
  ColorPickerInput,
  ColorPickerTrigger,
  Field,
  FieldControl,
  FieldDescription,
  FieldLabel,
} from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [sent, setSent] = useState("");

  return (
    <form
      style={{ display: "grid", gap: 16, justifyItems: "start" }}
      onSubmit={(event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        const data = new FormData(event.currentTarget);
        setSent(`Sent accent=${String(data.get("accent"))}`);
      }}
    >
      <Field>
        <FieldLabel>Accent color</FieldLabel>
        <ColorPicker name="accent" defaultValue="#7c3aed">
          <FieldControl>
            <ColorPickerTrigger />
          </FieldControl>
          <ColorPickerContent>
            <ColorPickerArea />
            <ColorPickerHue />
            <ColorPickerInput />
          </ColorPickerContent>
        </ColorPicker>
        <FieldDescription>Used for links and buttons.</FieldDescription>
      </Field>
      <Button type="submit">Save</Button>
      <p role="status" style={{ margin: 0, minBlockSize: 20, fontSize: 14 }}>
        {sent}
      </p>
    </form>
  );
}
```

