## While something is worked on

The shimmer is a look and says nothing by itself. To have a screen reader read the words when they appear, put them in an element with `role="status"` that's in the page from the start.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Button, TextShimmer } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [waiting, setWaiting] = useState(false);
  const [answer, setAnswer] = useState("");

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!waiting) return;
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      setWaiting(false);
      setAnswer("Revenue rose 12% over last quarter.");
    }, 3000);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [waiting]);

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "start" }}>
      <Button
        intent="secondary"
        disabled={waiting}
        onClick={() => {
          setAnswer("");
          setWaiting(true);
        }}
      >
        Summarize the quarter
      </Button>
      {/* In the page from the start, so a screen reader hears each change. */}
      <p role="status" style={{ margin: 0, minBlockSize: "1.5em" }}>
        {waiting ? <TextShimmer>Reading the report</TextShimmer> : answer}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Growing with the text

With `autoResize` the box is as tall as its text, so nothing scrolls out of sight. It shrinks again when text is deleted.

```tsx
import { Label, Textarea } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <div
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: 6,
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 400,
      }}
    >
      <Label htmlFor="textarea-reply">Reply</Label>
      <Textarea
        id="textarea-reply"
        autoResize
        rows={2}
        defaultValue="Thanks for the report. We found the cause and a fix is on its way."
        style={
          {
            "--nuv-textarea-min-height": "0px",
            "--nuv-textarea-max-height": "12rem",
          } as never
        }
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

Two variables set its limits. `--nuv-textarea-min-height` is the height it starts at, 5rem unless you change it. `--nuv-textarea-max-height` is where it stops growing and starts to scroll. There's no limit unless you set one.

Where the browser has the CSS property `field-sizing`, the stylesheet does this alone. Where it doesn't, the component measures the text and sets the height itself. The tests hold both to the same result.

Without `autoResize`, the corner of the box can be dragged to make it taller. It can't be dragged wider, which would push the page around it out of shape.

## Setup

There are two pieces. `Toaster` is the component that shows toasts, and `toast()` is the function that sends it one.

Render `Toaster` once, near the root of the app:

```tsx title="app/layout.tsx"
import { Toaster } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        {children}
        <Toaster />
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

Then call `toast()` from any client component. It doesn't need a hook, a context or a ref to the `Toaster`:

```tsx
"use client";

import { Button, toast } from "@nuvui/react";

export function SaveButton() {
  return <Button onClick={() => toast("Changes saved")}>Save changes</Button>;
}
```

Render only one `Toaster`. With two on the page, each would show every toast.

## With an action

A toast can carry one button. Pressing it runs `onClick` and closes the toast.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Button, toast } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [deleted, setDeleted] = useState(false);

  function remove() {
    setDeleted(true);
    toast("report.pdf deleted", {
      // Long enough to read it and get to the button.
      duration: 10000,
      action: {
        label: "Undo",
        altText: "Restore it from the trash",
        onClick: () => setDeleted(false),
      },
    });
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, justifyItems: "center" }}>
      <p style={{ margin: 0 }}>
        {deleted ? "The file is in the trash." : "report.pdf"}
      </p>
      <Button intent="danger" disabled={deleted} onClick={remove}>
        Delete file
      </Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`altText` is required. A screen reader user may not get to the button before the toast has gone, so it tells them another way to do the same thing.

## Changing a toast that's showing

Pass the id of a toast that's already up and it's replaced where it is, instead of a second one appearing. This suits anything with a start and a finish.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Button, toast } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  function upload() {
    // Stays until the same id is used again below.
    const id = toast("Uploading photo.jpg", { duration: Infinity });

    setTimeout(() => {
      toast("photo.jpg uploaded", { id, intent: "success" });
    }, 2000);
  }

  return <Button onClick={upload}>Upload a photo</Button>;
}
```

## Several at once

Three toasts show at a time. A fourth waits, and shows when one of the three closes. The button below sends five to show it happening.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Button, toast } from "@nuvui/react";

const messages = [
  "Draft saved",
  "Link copied",
  "Invitation sent",
  "Export ready",
  "Comment posted",
];

export default function Example() {
  function send() {
    for (const message of messages) toast(message);
  }

  return <Button onClick={send}>Send five toasts</Button>;
}
```

A toast that's waiting isn't on the page yet. Its clock starts when it shows, so it gets its full time, and that's also when a screen reader hears it.

Change the number with `limit` on `Toaster`. A toast with `duration: Infinity` holds its place until someone closes it, so three of those keep everything behind them waiting.

`toast.dismiss()` with no id clears the ones that are waiting as well as the ones showing.

## Sizes

```tsx
import { Toggle } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <Toggle variant="outline" size="sm">
        Small
      </Toggle>
      <Toggle variant="outline" size="md">
        Medium
      </Toggle>
      <Toggle variant="outline" size="lg">
        Large
      </Toggle>
    </>
  );
}
```

On a touch screen every size is at least 44 pixels tall and wide, and `lg` is 48. The smaller heights (32, 40 and 48 pixels) only apply when a mouse or trackpad is the main input, and [density](page:theming#density) moves them.

## Several at once

```tsx
import { ToggleGroup, ToggleGroupItem } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <ToggleGroup
      type="multiple"
      variant="ghost"
      aria-label="Text style"
      defaultValue={["bold"]}
    >
      <ToggleGroupItem value="bold">Bold</ToggleGroupItem>
      <ToggleGroupItem value="italic">Italic</ToggleGroupItem>
      <ToggleGroupItem value="underline">Underline</ToggleGroupItem>
    </ToggleGroup>
  );
}
```

`variant="ghost"` leaves a small gap between the buttons and gives none of them an edge. The default, `"outline"`, joins them into one strip.

## Always one pressed

With `type="single"`, pressing the pressed button again lets go of it, and the group has no value. For a view switcher that should always have one, ignore the empty value.

```tsx
"use client";

import { ToggleGroup, ToggleGroupItem } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

export default function Example() {
  const [align, setAlign] = useState("left");

  return (
    <ToggleGroup
      type="single"
      aria-label="Alignment"
      value={align}
      // Radix sends an empty string when the pressed button is pressed
      // again. Ignoring it keeps one of them always picked.
      onValueChange={(value) => {
        if (value) setAlign(value);
      }}
    >
      <ToggleGroupItem value="left">Left</ToggleGroupItem>
      <ToggleGroupItem value="center">Center</ToggleGroupItem>
      <ToggleGroupItem value="right">Right</ToggleGroupItem>
    </ToggleGroup>
  );
}
```

## CSS variables

The buttons read the [toggle's variables](page:components/toggle#css-variables). Set them on the group and every button in it follows. The group adds one of its own.

```css
.toolbar {
  --nuv-toggle-on-bg: var(--color-foreground);
  --nuv-toggle-on-fg: var(--color-background);
}
```

| Variable | Default | What it sets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--nuv-toggle-group-gap` | `var(--spacing)` | Space between buttons in the ghost variant. |

## Vertical

`orientation="vertical"` stacks the controls, turns the separators on their side, and moves focus with the up and down arrows.

```tsx
import { Toolbar, ToolbarButton, ToolbarSeparator } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Toolbar orientation="vertical" aria-label="Drawing tools">
      <ToolbarButton>Select</ToolbarButton>
      <ToolbarButton>Pen</ToolbarButton>
      <ToolbarButton>Shape</ToolbarButton>
      <ToolbarSeparator />
      <ToolbarButton>Erase</ToolbarButton>
    </Toolbar>
  );
}
```

## Import

```tsx
import {
  Tooltip,
  TooltipContent,
  TooltipProvider,
  TooltipTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";
```

The styles come from `@nuvui/react/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app. [Getting started](page:index) covers that, and how to load only this component's CSS.

## Several tooltips

On its own, each tooltip waits out its delay. Put a group inside one `TooltipProvider` and only the first one waits. Moving on to the next within a moment shows it at once, which is what you want in a toolbar.

```tsx
import {
  Button,
  Tooltip,
  TooltipContent,
  TooltipProvider,
  TooltipTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";

const tools = [
  { name: "Bold", keys: "Ctrl+B", glyph: "B", weight: 700 },
  { name: "Italic", keys: "Ctrl+I", glyph: "I", weight: 400 },
  { name: "Underline", keys: "Ctrl+U", glyph: "U", weight: 400 },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      {tools.map((tool) => (
        <Tooltip key={tool.name}>
          <TooltipTrigger asChild>
            <Button
              intent="secondary"
              aria-label={tool.name}
              style={{ fontWeight: tool.weight }}
            >
              {tool.glyph}
            </Button>
          </TooltipTrigger>
          <TooltipContent>{tool.keys}</TooltipContent>
        </Tooltip>
      ))}
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
}
```

Outside a provider, a tooltip still works. It brings its own.

## Sides

```tsx
import {
  Button,
  Tooltip,
  TooltipContent,
  TooltipProvider,
  TooltipTrigger,
} from "@nuvui/react";

const sides = ["top", "right", "bottom", "left"] as const;

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      {sides.map((side) => (
        <Tooltip key={side}>
          <TooltipTrigger asChild>
            <Button intent="secondary">{side}</Button>
          </TooltipTrigger>
          <TooltipContent side={side}>Opens on the {side}</TooltipContent>
        </Tooltip>
      ))}
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
}
```

