## Install

```sh
pnpm add @nuvui/chat @nuvui/react
```

## Import

```tsx
import {
  Conversation,
  ConversationContent,
  ConversationEmpty,
  ConversationScrollButton,
} from "@nuvui/chat";
```

The styles come from `@nuvui/chat/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app, after `@nuvui/react/styles.css`. [Chat](page:chat/index) covers that.

```tsx
<Conversation style={{ blockSize: 480 }}>
  <ConversationContent aria-label="Billing questions">
    {/* messages */}
  </ConversationContent>
  <ConversationScrollButton />
  {/* the prompt box */}
</Conversation>
```

## Parts

| Part | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `Conversation` | The whole: a column, as tall as you make it. |
| `ConversationContent` | The messages. This is the box that scrolls, and it takes the room that's left. |
| `ConversationScrollButton` | A button over the end of the messages that goes to the latest one. It's only there while the latest is out of view. |
| `ConversationEmpty` | What's shown before any message, in the middle of the room. |

Whatever else you put in the `Conversation`, such as a [prompt box](page:chat/prompt-input), sits under the messages at its own height.

## Give it a height

A conversation is as tall as you make it, and the messages scroll inside. With no height it grows with its messages and nothing scrolls. Set one on it, or put it in something that has one:

```css
.chat {
  block-size: 100dvh;
}
```

`dvh` follows a phone's address bar and its keyboard, so the prompt box stays in view while someone types.

## Following a reply

It starts at the latest message. While that's in view, anything that makes the messages taller keeps it in view: a reply arriving word by word, a new message, a picture that loads.

When the reader scrolls up, it stops. New messages arrive below without moving what they're reading, and the button appears. It follows again once they scroll back to the end, or press the button.

Sending a message from a `PromptInput` inside the conversation goes to the end as well, wherever the reader was: they've just written something and want to see the answer.

## Before the first message

`ConversationEmpty` holds a greeting and some [prompts to start from](page:chat/prompt-suggestion). Render it in place of the messages while there are none.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Conversation,
  ConversationContent,
  ConversationEmpty,
  Message,
  MessageContent,
  PromptInput,
  PromptInputSubmit,
  PromptInputTextarea,
  PromptInputToolbar,
  PromptSuggestion,
  PromptSuggestions,
} from "@nuvui/chat";
import { useState } from "react";

const starters = [
  "Summarize last month's invoices",
  "Who hasn't paid yet?",
  "Draft a reminder",
];

export default function Example() {
  const [sent, setSent] = useState<string[]>([]);

  return (
    <Conversation
      style={{
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 520,
        blockSize: 380,
        border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
        borderRadius: "var(--radius-xl)",
      }}
    >
      <ConversationContent aria-label="A new conversation">
        {sent.length === 0 ? (
          <ConversationEmpty>
            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontWeight: 600 }}>
              What do you want to know?
            </p>
            <PromptSuggestions>
              {starters.map((text) => (
                <PromptSuggestion
                  key={text}
                  onClick={() => setSent((now) => [...now, text])}
                >
                  {text}
                </PromptSuggestion>
              ))}
            </PromptSuggestions>
          </ConversationEmpty>
        ) : (
          sent.map((text, index) => (
            <Message key={index} from="user">
              <MessageContent>{text}</MessageContent>
            </Message>
          ))
        )}
      </ConversationContent>
      <div style={{ padding: "0 1rem 1rem" }}>
        <PromptInput onSubmit={(text) => setSent((now) => [...now, text])}>
          <PromptInputTextarea
            aria-label="Message"
            placeholder="Ask anything"
          />
          <PromptInputToolbar>
            <PromptInputSubmit />
          </PromptInputToolbar>
        </PromptInput>
      </div>
    </Conversation>
  );
}
```

## From a part of your own

`useConversation` gives whether the latest message is in view and a function that goes to it, for something of yours inside a `Conversation`, such as a "3 new messages" pill.

```tsx
function NewMessages({ count }: { count: number }) {
  const { atBottom, scrollToBottom } = useConversation();
  if (atBottom || count === 0) return null;
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={() => scrollToBottom()}>
      {count} new
    </button>
  );
}
```

`scrollToBottom({ behavior: "auto" })` jumps there without gliding.

## ConversationScrollButton props

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `label` | `string` | `"Go to the latest message"` | The button's name for a screen reader. |
| `children` | `ReactNode` |  | In place of the arrow. |

`Conversation`, `ConversationContent` and `ConversationEmpty` take the attributes of a `div`. All four pass `ref` to their element.

## Accessibility

### Keyboard

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| <kbd>Tab</kbd> | Stops at the messages, so they can be scrolled, then at anything in them that takes focus. |
| <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> <kbd>Page Up</kbd> <kbd>Page Down</kbd> <kbd>Home</kbd> <kbd>End</kbd> | With focus on the messages, scroll them. |

### What the component handles

- `ConversationContent` has `role="log"`, so a screen reader reads a message when it's added and doesn't read the others again.
- It can take focus, which is what lets it be scrolled from the keyboard.
- The button to go to the latest message is named, and removed while there's nowhere to go. Pressing it puts focus on the messages, so focus isn't dropped when the button goes.
- For someone who has asked for less motion, going to the end is a jump and not a glide.

### What you need to handle

- Name the messages with `aria-label` on `ConversationContent`: "Conversation with support", say.
- Mark a reply that's still arriving with `streaming` on its [`Message`](page:chat/message), so that it's read once and whole.
- Keep the newest message last in the markup. A log is read in the order it's written.

### What hasn't been checked

The tests check the role, the names and that focus lands where it should. What each screen reader says as messages arrive hasn't been checked by a person.

## On small screens

The messages have 16 pixels of space around them and are never wider than 48rem. The button to go to the end is 44 pixels on a touch screen and 36 with a mouse.

## CSS variables

Set any of these on the `Conversation`, or on anything around it.

```css
.chat {
  --nuv-conversation-max-width: 40rem;
  --nuv-conversation-gap: 2rem;
}
```

| Variable | Default | What it sets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--nuv-conversation-fg` | `var(--color-foreground)` | Text color. |
| `--nuv-conversation-gap` | `calc(var(--spacing) * 6)` | Space between two messages. |
| `--nuv-conversation-max-width` | `48rem` | The most width the messages take. They sit in the middle of a wider box. |
| `--nuv-conversation-padding` | `calc(var(--spacing) * 4)` | Space around the messages, inside the box that scrolls. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-size` | `var(--nuv-touch-size)`, `calc(var(--spacing) * 9)` | The button's width and height. Larger by default with a finger than with a mouse. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-offset` | `calc(var(--spacing) * 3)` | How far above the end of the messages the button sits. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-border` | `var(--color-border-strong)` | Color of that button's edge. Keep it at 3:1 against the messages' background. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-bg` | `var(--color-background)` | Background of the button that goes to the end. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-fg` | `var(--color-foreground)` | Color of its arrow. |
| `--nuv-conversation-scroll-button-hover-bg` | `var(--color-muted)` | Its background under the pointer. |

## Classes

| Class | What it's on |
| --- | --- |
| `.nuv-conversation` | Conversation |
| `.nuv-conversation__content` | The column of messages inside it. |
| `.nuv-conversation__empty` | ConversationEmpty |
| `.nuv-conversation__scroll-button` | ConversationScrollButton |
| `.nuv-conversation__viewport` | ConversationContent: the box that scrolls. |

## Server components

Conversation starts with `"use client"`: it follows the scrolling. A server component can render it with messages written there. A conversation that changes is a client component of yours anyway, since it holds the messages.

## Do and don't

**Do**

- Give it a height, from the page or from what it's in.
- Put the prompt box inside the `Conversation`, after the messages, so sending goes to the end.

**Don't**

- Don't scroll to the end yourself when a message arrives. That takes the page away from someone reading further up, which is what this is for.
- Don't put the newest message first. It follows the end.

sections.markdown:
## Give it a height

A conversation is as tall as you make it, and the messages scroll inside. With no height it grows with its messages and nothing scrolls. Set one on it, or put it in something that has one:

```css
.chat {
  block-size: 100dvh;
}
```

`dvh` follows a phone's address bar and its keyboard, so the prompt box stays in view while someone types.

## Before the first message

`ConversationEmpty` holds a greeting and some [prompts to start from](page:chat/prompt-suggestion). Render it in place of the messages while there are none.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Conversation,
  ConversationContent,
  ConversationEmpty,
  Message,
  MessageContent,
  PromptInput,
  PromptInputSubmit,
  PromptInputTextarea,
  PromptInputToolbar,
  PromptSuggestion,
  PromptSuggestions,
} from "@nuvui/chat";
import { useState } from "react";

const starters = [
  "Summarize last month's invoices",
  "Who hasn't paid yet?",
  "Draft a reminder",
];

export default function Example() {
  const [sent, setSent] = useState<string[]>([]);

  return (
    <Conversation
      style={{
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 520,
        blockSize: 380,
        border: "1px solid var(--color-border)",
        borderRadius: "var(--radius-xl)",
      }}
    >
      <ConversationContent aria-label="A new conversation">
        {sent.length === 0 ? (
          <ConversationEmpty>
            <p style={{ margin: 0, fontWeight: 600 }}>
              What do you want to know?
            </p>
            <PromptSuggestions>
              {starters.map((text) => (
                <PromptSuggestion
                  key={text}
                  onClick={() => setSent((now) => [...now, text])}
                >
                  {text}
                </PromptSuggestion>
              ))}
            </PromptSuggestions>
          </ConversationEmpty>
        ) : (
          sent.map((text, index) => (
            <Message key={index} from="user">
              <MessageContent>{text}</MessageContent>
            </Message>
          ))
        )}
      </ConversationContent>
      <div style={{ padding: "0 1rem 1rem" }}>
        <PromptInput onSubmit={(text) => setSent((now) => [...now, text])}>
          <PromptInputTextarea
            aria-label="Message"
            placeholder="Ask anything"
          />
          <PromptInputToolbar>
            <PromptInputSubmit />
          </PromptInputToolbar>
        </PromptInput>
      </div>
    </Conversation>
  );
}
```

## From a part of your own

`useConversation` gives whether the latest message is in view and a function that goes to it, for something of yours inside a `Conversation`, such as a "3 new messages" pill.

```tsx
function NewMessages({ count }: { count: number }) {
  const { atBottom, scrollToBottom } = useConversation();
  if (atBottom || count === 0) return null;
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={() => scrollToBottom()}>
      {count} new
    </button>
  );
}
```

`scrollToBottom({ behavior: "auto" })` jumps there without gliding.

## A picture and a name

`avatar` puts a picture beside the message, and a `MessageMeta` puts a line over it. Each message is already announced with who it's from. When the name is on the page, in the line, pass `label={null}` so it isn't said twice.

```tsx
import { Message, MessageContent, MessageMeta } from "@nuvui/chat";
import { Avatar, AvatarFallback } from "@nuvui/react";

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <div
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: "1.5rem",
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 520,
      }}
    >
      <Message
        from="user"
        label={null}
        avatar={
          <Avatar>
            <AvatarFallback>AL</AvatarFallback>
          </Avatar>
        }
      >
        <MessageMeta>Ada Lovelace · 09:41</MessageMeta>
        <MessageContent>Is the March report ready?</MessageContent>
      </Message>
      <Message
        from="assistant"
        label={null}
        avatar={
          <Avatar>
            <AvatarFallback>N</AvatarFallback>
          </Avatar>
        }
      >
        <MessageMeta>Northwind assistant · 09:41</MessageMeta>
        <MessageContent>
          It is. Revenue is up 8% on February, and two invoices are overdue.
        </MessageContent>
      </Message>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## A reply that's still arriving

Set `streaming` while a reply is being written and take it off when it's done. Nothing looks different. It tells a screen reader to wait and read the message once it's whole, not each piece as it arrives. Before the first words, show a [`MessageLoader`](page:chat/message-loader) where they'll be.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Message, MessageContent, MessageLoader } from "@nuvui/chat";
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

const words =
  "Revenue for March was 48,200, up 8% on February. Most of the rise came from three new customers on the Team plan.".split(
    " ",
  );

export default function Example() {
  // How many words have arrived. None yet is the wait before a reply.
  const [count, setCount] = useState(words.length);
  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setInterval>>(undefined);
  useEffect(() => () => clearInterval(timer.current), []);

  function again() {
    clearInterval(timer.current);
    setCount(0);
    timer.current = setInterval(() => {
      setCount((now) => {
        if (now + 1 >= words.length) clearInterval(timer.current);
        return Math.min(now + 1, words.length);
      });
    }, 150);
  }

  const streaming = count < words.length;

  return (
    <div
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: "1rem",
        justifyItems: "start",
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 520,
      }}
    >
      <Message from="assistant" streaming={streaming}>
        {count === 0 ? (
          <MessageLoader />
        ) : (
          <MessageContent>{words.slice(0, count).join(" ")}</MessageContent>
        )}
      </Message>
      <Button intent="secondary" disabled={streaming} onClick={again}>
        Reply again
      </Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Actions

`MessageActions` is a row of buttons under a message. Each `MessageAction` is an icon with a `label`, which is its name for a screen reader and the text of its tooltip. `MessageCopyAction` copies `text` and shows a tick for two seconds.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Message,
  MessageAction,
  MessageActions,
  MessageContent,
  MessageCopyAction,
} from "@nuvui/chat";
import { useState } from "react";

const text =
  "Two invoices are overdue: INV-1042 for Contoso, 12 days, and INV-1057 for Fabrikam, 3 days.";

const icon = {
  width: 16,
  height: 16,
  viewBox: "0 0 16 16",
  fill: "none",
  stroke: "currentColor",
  strokeWidth: 1.5,
  strokeLinecap: "round",
  strokeLinejoin: "round",
} as const;

export default function Example() {
  const [tries, setTries] = useState(1);

  return (
    <div style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 520 }}>
      <Message from="assistant">
        <MessageContent>{text}</MessageContent>
        <MessageActions>
          <MessageCopyAction text={text} />
          <MessageAction
            label="Try again"
            onClick={() => setTries((now) => now + 1)}
          >
            <svg aria-hidden="true" {...icon}>
              <path d="M13.5 8a5.5 5.5 0 1 1-1.6-3.9M13.5 2.5v2.5H11" />
            </svg>
          </MessageAction>
        </MessageActions>
      </Message>
      <p role="status" style={{ marginBlock: "0.75rem 0" }}>
        Answer {tries}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

The buttons are always there. Showing them only under the pointer would hide them from a touch screen and from the keyboard.

Copying needs a page served over HTTPS, or `localhost`. Where the browser refuses, `onCopyError` is called and nothing is shown as copied.

pages.markdown:
# Markdown

A reply's markdown drawn with the library's own type. Headings, lists, tables, links and code, safe to give text a model wrote, and able to take text that's still arriving.

Use it for a reply. A model writes markdown, and shown as plain text that's a page of asterisks and backticks. This draws it, with the same type, colors and spacing as the rest of your app.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Message, MessageContent } from "@nuvui/chat";
import { Markdown } from "@nuvui/chat/markdown";
import { createShikiHighlighter } from "@nuvui/chat/shiki";

// One for the whole app, made outside any component.
const highlighter = createShikiHighlighter();

const reply = `## Two invoices are overdue

Both are for **Contoso**. The older one is past the day a [late fee](#late-fees) starts.

| Invoice | Amount | Days late |
| :-- | --: | --: |
| INV-1042 | 4,200 | 12 |
| INV-1057 | 1,150 | 3 |

What I'd do next:

1. Send a reminder for \`INV-1042\` today.
2. Leave \`INV-1057\` until Friday.

To find them yourself:

\`\`\`ts
const overdue = invoices.filter(
  (invoice) => invoice.customer === "Contoso" && invoice.daysLate > 0,
);
\`\`\`
`;

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <div style={{ inlineSize: "100%", maxInlineSize: 560 }}>
      <Message from="assistant">
        <MessageContent>
          <Markdown highlighter={highlighter} headingOffset={1}>
            {reply}
          </Markdown>
        </MessageContent>
      </Message>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Install

```sh
pnpm add @nuvui/chat @nuvui/react
```

## Import

```tsx
import { Markdown } from "@nuvui/chat/markdown";
```

It has an entry of its own and isn't exported from `@nuvui/chat`. It brings a markdown parser, [react-markdown](https://github.com/remarkjs/react-markdown) with [remark-gfm](https://github.com/remarkjs/remark-gfm), which is about 42 kB compressed. An app that imports the other parts and not this one doesn't load it.

The styles come from `@nuvui/chat/styles.css`, which you load once for the whole app, after `@nuvui/react/styles.css`. [Chat](page:chat/index) covers that.

```tsx
<Message from="assistant" streaming={replying}>
  <MessageContent>
    <Markdown streaming={replying}>{message.text}</Markdown>
  </MessageContent>
</Message>
```

## What it draws

Everything in CommonMark, and GitHub's additions to it: tables, task lists, struck text and addresses that become links by themselves.

A fenced block of code is a [`CodeBlock`](page:chat/code-block), with its language over it and a button that copies it. A table that's wider than the message scrolls in a box of its own.

## Text a model wrote is not trusted

What a model writes can be steered by what it read: a web page, a file, an earlier message. So this treats its text as something to show and nothing more.

- **HTML in the text is shown as text.** A `<script>` or an `<img onerror>` is drawn as the characters it's made of, and never becomes an element.
- **Addresses are checked.** A link to `javascript:` or another scheme that runs something loses its address.
- **Pictures aren't loaded unless you say so.** A picture is fetched the moment it's drawn, with nobody pressing anything, and its address can carry whatever the model was talked into putting there, a piece of the conversation included. So a picture is drawn as a link, with its description as the text. `allowImages` loads them. Turn it on where you know where the text comes from, or where your server limits which hosts a picture may load from.

## A reply that's still arriving

Markdown that stops halfway is drawn as loose marks: a block of code is a paragraph with three backticks in front of it until its last line arrives. Set `streaming` while the text arrives, and what's open at its end is closed for the moment.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Message, MessageContent } from "@nuvui/chat";
import { Markdown } from "@nuvui/chat/markdown";
import { Button, Switch } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

const reply = `A reminder goes out on **day 31**, and a late fee starts on ~~day 40~~ **day 45**.

In code, with \`dueDays\` as the invoice's own terms:

\`\`\`ts
function isLate(sentOn: Date, dueDays = 30) {
  const due = new Date(sentOn);
  due.setDate(due.getDate() + dueDays);
  return Date.now() > due.getTime();
}
\`\`\`

The [payment terms](#payment-terms) have the rest.`;

export default function Example() {
  // How many characters have arrived.
  const [arrived, setArrived] = useState(reply.length);
  const [closing, setClosing] = useState(true);
  const writing = arrived < reply.length;

  // Stands in for a model: four characters every 40 milliseconds.
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!writing) return;
    const timer = setInterval(
      () => setArrived((now) => Math.min(now + 4, reply.length)),
      40,
    );
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, [writing]);

  return (
    <div
      style={{
        display: "grid",
        gap: "0.75rem",
        justifyItems: "start",
        inlineSize: "100%",
        maxInlineSize: 560,
      }}
    >
      <Message
        from="assistant"
        streaming={writing}
        style={{ inlineSize: "100%", minBlockSize: 300 }}
      >
        <MessageContent style={{ inlineSize: "100%" }}>
          <Markdown streaming={writing && closing}>
            {reply.slice(0, arrived)}
          </Markdown>
        </MessageContent>
      </Message>
      <div style={{ display: "flex", flexWrap: "wrap", gap: "1rem" }}>
        <Button
          intent="secondary"
          size="sm"
          disabled={writing}
          onClick={() => setArrived(0)}
        >
          Write it again
        </Button>
        <div style={{ display: "flex", gap: "0.5rem", alignItems: "center" }}>
          <Switch
            id="close-what-is-open"
            checked={closing}
            onCheckedChange={setClosing}
          />
          <label htmlFor="close-what-is-open">
            Close what's open while it arrives
          </label>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

It closes fenced code, code in a line, `**bold**` and `~~struck~~`, and shows a link whose address is still arriving as its text. It leaves italics alone: a single `*` or `_` is as often a bullet, a product of two numbers or part of a name. The function that does it is exported as `closeMarkdown`, for text you draw another way.

The whole text is read again each time it changes. For a reply of an ordinary length, a few times a second, that isn't noticed. A message that isn't changing isn't read again.

## Colored code

Give it a `highlighter` and the blocks of code are colored. A ready one for Shiki is at `@nuvui/chat/shiki`. [CodeBlock](page:chat/code-block#colors) covers it.

```tsx
import { createShikiHighlighter } from "@nuvui/chat/shiki";

// Once, outside any component.
const highlighter = createShikiHighlighter();

<Markdown highlighter={highlighter}>{text}</Markdown>;
```

## Headings

A reply's `#` is an `h1`, and your page has one already. `headingOffset` moves a reply's headings down: with `2`, `#` is an `h3` and `##` an `h4`. They don't go past `h6`.

## Your own elements

`components` replaces an element with one of yours, by tag name, the way react-markdown takes them. Links that open in a new tab, for one:

```tsx
const components = {
  a: ({ node, ...props }) => <a {...props} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" />,
};

<Markdown components={components}>{text}</Markdown>;
```

Keep the object outside the component, or in a `useMemo`. A new object on every render makes every element a new one, and a block of code would lose its colors each time.

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `children` (required) | `string` |  | The markdown. |
| `streaming` | `boolean` | `false` | The text is still arriving. What's open at its end is closed for the moment, so a block of code is drawn as one before its last line has come, and bold as bold. |
| `highlighter` | `CodeHighlighter` |  | Colors the blocks of code. Without one they're in one color. A ready one for Shiki is at `@nuvui/chat/shiki`. Make it once, outside the component: a new one on every render colors everything again. |
| `allowImages` | `boolean` | `false` | Whether pictures in the text are loaded. Left off, a picture is a link with its description as the text. A picture's address is fetched as soon as it's drawn, and text a model wrote can put anything in that address, the conversation included. |
| `headingOffset` | `number` | `0` | How many levels the headings are moved down, so that they fit under the page's own. With `2`, `#` is an `h3`. |
| `components` | `Components` |  | Your own elements in place of these, by tag name, as react-markdown takes them. Keep the object the same from one render to the next. |
| `tableLabel` | `string` | `"Table"` | The name of the box a wide table scrolls in. |
| `doneLabel` | `string` | `"Done"` | What a ticked item of a task list is called. |
| `todoLabel` | `string` | `"To do"` | What one that isn't ticked is called. |
| `copyLabel` | `string` | `"Copy code"` | The name of the button that copies a block of code. |
| `copiedLabel` | `string` | `"Copied"` | Its name for the two seconds after it has copied. |
| `codeLabel` | `string` | `"Code"` | The name of the box a block of code scrolls in. |

It also takes the attributes of a `div`, and passes `ref` to it.

## Accessibility

### What the component handles

- Headings, lists, tables and quotes are the real elements, so a screen reader can move by them.
- A table's box, and a block of code, can take focus when they scroll, and are named "Table" and "Code".
- A task list's box says "Done" or "To do", since nothing else names it.
- A link is underlined. It's told from the text around it by more than a color.
- A picture that isn't loaded is a link with the picture's description as its text.

### What you need to handle

- Put it in a [`Message`](page:chat/message) that has `streaming` while the reply arrives, so a screen reader reads it once it's whole.
- Set `headingOffset` so a reply's headings fit under your page's.
- Translate `tableLabel`, `doneLabel`, `todoLabel`, `copyLabel`, `copiedLabel` and `codeLabel`.
- A model writes what it writes. Headings that skip a level, or a picture with no description, come through as they are.

## CSS variables

Set any of these on a `Markdown`, or on anything around it.

```css
.chat {
  --nuv-markdown-gap: calc(var(--spacing) * 4);
  --nuv-markdown-link-fg: var(--color-primary);
}
```

| Variable | Default | What it sets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--nuv-markdown-gap` | `calc(var(--spacing) * 3)` | Space between paragraphs, lists and the other parts. |
| `--nuv-markdown-fg` | `inherit` | Color of the text. It's the message's unless you set it. |
| `--nuv-markdown-link-fg` | `inherit` | Color of a link. Keep it at 4.5:1. The default theme's primary color is checked as a control's color, at 3:1, and not as text. |
| `--nuv-markdown-quote-border` | `var(--color-border-strong)` | Color of the line beside a quote. |
| `--nuv-markdown-quote-fg` | `var(--color-muted-foreground)` | Color of a quote's text. Keep it at 4.5:1. |
| `--nuv-markdown-border` | `var(--color-border)` | Color of a table's lines and of a rule. |
| `--nuv-markdown-code-bg` | `var(--color-muted)` | Background of code in a line. |
| `--nuv-markdown-code-fg` | `var(--color-foreground)` | Color of code in a line. Keep it at 4.5:1 against its background. |
| `--nuv-markdown-table-head-bg` | `var(--color-muted)` | Background of a table's heading row. |

## Classes

| Class | What it's on |
| --- | --- |
| `.nuv-markdown` | Markdown |
| `.nuv-markdown__table` | The box a table scrolls in. |

What's inside has no classes: it's styled by its element, `h2`, `ul`, `table`, each in `:where()`, which adds nothing to how strong the rule is. A rule of yours with one class wins.

## Server components

Markdown starts with `"use client"`. A server component can render it with text and no function: without `highlighter`, and without `components`.

## Do and don't

**Do**

- Set `streaming` for exactly as long as the text arrives.
- Make the highlighter once for the whole app.

**Don't**

- Don't turn `allowImages` on for text you can't vouch for.
- Don't use it for a page of your own writing. That's a build step's job, where the text is read once and not in the browser.

