# MapRoute

A line on a map through a list of places, for a way that was driven or one that's planned. It's drawn in the theme's colors, with an edge that keeps it seen on a map of any color.

Use a route for a way from one place to another: where a van has been, what a walk covers, which stops a line calls at. It draws the line and doesn't find it. The places come from you, which mostly means from a routing service, your map provider's or another.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  MapControls,
  type MapLngLat,
  MapMarker,
  MapRoute,
  MapView,
  mapBoundsOf,
} from "@nuvui/map";

const style = "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty";

// Made up. The line your routing service gives goes here: this library
// draws a way, and doesn't find one.
const driven: MapLngLat[] = [
  [4.8952, 52.3702],
  [4.8936, 52.3667],
  [4.8905, 52.3631],
  [4.8882, 52.3598],
];
const planned: MapLngLat[] = [
  [4.8882, 52.3598],
  [4.8911, 52.3564],
  [4.8972, 52.3541],
];

const stops: { name: string; at: MapLngLat }[] = [
  { name: "Depot, where the van set out", at: [4.8952, 52.3702] },
  { name: "The van, at Leidseplein", at: [4.8882, 52.3598] },
  { name: "Last stop, Sarphatipark", at: [4.8972, 52.3541] },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <MapView
      aria-label="Van 12 on its round"
      mapStyle={style}
      // Every place on the way, in view.
      bounds={mapBoundsOf([...driven, ...planned])}
      boundsPadding={64}
      cooperativeGestures
    >
      <MapRoute coordinates={driven} />
      <MapRoute coordinates={planned} dashed />
      {stops.map((stop) => (
        <MapMarker key={stop.name} position={stop.at} label={stop.name} />
      ))}
      <MapControls />
    </MapView>
  );
}
```

## Install

```sh
pnpm add @nuvui/map maplibre-gl @nuvui/react
```

## Import

```tsx
import { MapRoute, MapView, mapBoundsOf } from "@nuvui/map";
```

A route goes inside a [MapView](page:map/map-view), which is where the styles, the worker and the provider are covered.

```tsx
<MapView aria-label="The way to the office" mapStyle={style}>
  <MapRoute
    coordinates={[
      [13.3777, 52.5163],
      [13.3888, 52.5105],
      [13.4050, 52.5200],
    ]}
  />
</MapView>
```

`coordinates` is the places the line goes through, in order, each as longitude and then latitude. A straight line is drawn from each to the next, so a way along roads needs a place at every bend, which is what a routing service gives.

## Showing all of it

`mapBoundsOf` takes a list of places and gives back the area that holds them, for the map's `bounds`. With it the map opens on the whole route, whatever size the map is.

```tsx
<MapView
  aria-label="Van 12 on its round"
  mapStyle={style}
  bounds={mapBoundsOf(way)}
  boundsPadding={64}
>
  <MapRoute coordinates={way} />
</MapView>
```

It works on any list of places, so it's also the one for "show every marker".

## Driven and planned

`dashed` draws the line in dashes. Two routes, one after the other, show a way that's part done: the first for what's behind, the second with `dashed` for what's ahead. The example at the top does that.

`width` is how thick the line is, in pixels.

## Colors

The line is the primary color, with an edge in the color that goes on it. Both are CSS variables, `--nuv-map-route-color` and `--nuv-map-route-edge`, and they take a color of the theme's.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  MapControls,
  type MapLngLat,
  MapRoute,
  MapView,
  mapBoundsOf,
} from "@nuvui/map";
import type { CSSProperties } from "react";

const style = "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty";

// Made up: two lines of a tram, each in its own color.
const lines: { name: string; color: string; way: MapLngLat[] }[] = [
  {
    name: "Line 2",
    color: "var(--color-success)",
    way: [
      [4.8759, 52.3584],
      [4.8829, 52.3622],
      [4.8898, 52.3667],
      [4.8952, 52.3738],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Line 12",
    color: "var(--color-danger)",
    way: [
      [4.8721, 52.3721],
      [4.8829, 52.3622],
      [4.8911, 52.3564],
      [4.9021, 52.3524],
    ],
  },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, inlineSize: "100%" }}>
      <MapView
        aria-label="Tram lines 2 and 12"
        mapStyle={style}
        bounds={mapBoundsOf(lines.flatMap((line) => line.way))}
        boundsPadding={48}
        cooperativeGestures
      >
        {lines.map((line) => (
          <MapRoute
            key={line.name}
            coordinates={line.way}
            width={5}
            // A color of the theme's, by its variable.
            style={{ "--nuv-map-route-color": line.color } as CSSProperties}
          />
        ))}
        <MapControls />
      </MapView>
      {/* A color on a map says nothing to someone who can't see it, so the
          lines are in words as well. */}
      <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: "var(--text-sm)" }}>
        Line 2 runs from Vondelpark to Centraal. Line 12 runs from Westerpark to
        Amstel, and crosses line 2 at Leidseplein.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Set them on the route itself with `style` or a `className`, or on anything around the map. They're read when the route is drawn, and again when its props or the page's theme change.

Don't let the color be the only thing that tells two routes apart. Name them in words beside the map.

## Under the place names

A route is drawn over everything in the map's style, names of streets included. `before` takes the name of a layer in the style and draws the route under it, which keeps the names readable over a thick line.

```tsx
<MapRoute coordinates={way} before="road_label" />
```

The names of the layers are the style's own, so they differ from one provider to the next. Your provider's style lists them. A name the style doesn't have leaves the route on top.

## Stops along it

A route has no markers. Put a [MapMarker](page:map/map-view#markers) at each place that matters: the start, the end, the stops.

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | What it's for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `coordinates` (required) | `MapLngLat[]` |  | The places the line goes through, in order. |
| `width` | `number` | `4` | How thick the line is, in pixels. |
| `dashed` | `boolean` | `false` | Draws it in dashes, for a way that's planned and not yet gone. |
| `before` | `string` |  | The name of a layer in the map's style to draw it under, such as the style's first layer of place names. Left out, it's drawn over the map. |

It renders a `span` that isn't drawn, which is where the colors are read from, and takes what a `span` takes, including `ref`, `className` and `style`. The line itself is drawn by MapLibre on the map's canvas.

### mapBoundsOf

```ts
function mapBoundsOf(places: MapLngLat[]): MapBounds | undefined;
```

The south-west and north-east corners of the area that holds every place. An empty list gives `undefined`, which leaves the map where it is.

## Accessibility

A line on a canvas is a picture, and isn't read. Nothing on it takes the focus.

### What the component handles

- The line has an edge in a second color, so it stands off a map of any color.

### What you need to handle

- Say in words what the route shows: where it starts and ends, the stops in order, how far along it is. A list beside the map does it. The [delivery route block](https://nuvui.nuvraxis.com/blocks/data) is a list and a map of the same stops.
- Give the map an `aria-label` that says whose way it is.
- Don't tell routes apart by color alone.

## On small screens

The line is as thick on a phone as on a desk. With `bounds` from `mapBoundsOf`, the whole route is in view at any width.

## CSS variables

The route's variables are in the [MapView](page:map/map-view#css-variables) page's list with the rest of the map's: `--nuv-map-route-color` and `--nuv-map-route-edge`.

```css
.late {
  --nuv-map-route-color: var(--color-danger);
}
```

## Server components

`MapRoute` starts with `"use client"`. It's drawn once there's a map, which is in a browser, so it isn't in what a server sends.

## Do and don't

**Do**

- Pass the places a routing service gives, with one at every bend.
- Use `dashed` for what's planned, beside a solid line for what's done.
- Put the stops in words beside the map.

**Don't**

- Don't draw a straight line between two towns and call it a way to drive.
- Don't use more colors than someone can keep apart. Past three or four routes, let the reader choose which to show.

sections.markdown:
## Colors

The line is the primary color, with an edge in the color that goes on it. Both are CSS variables, `--nuv-map-route-color` and `--nuv-map-route-edge`, and they take a color of the theme's.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  MapControls,
  type MapLngLat,
  MapRoute,
  MapView,
  mapBoundsOf,
} from "@nuvui/map";
import type { CSSProperties } from "react";

const style = "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty";

// Made up: two lines of a tram, each in its own color.
const lines: { name: string; color: string; way: MapLngLat[] }[] = [
  {
    name: "Line 2",
    color: "var(--color-success)",
    way: [
      [4.8759, 52.3584],
      [4.8829, 52.3622],
      [4.8898, 52.3667],
      [4.8952, 52.3738],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Line 12",
    color: "var(--color-danger)",
    way: [
      [4.8721, 52.3721],
      [4.8829, 52.3622],
      [4.8911, 52.3564],
      [4.9021, 52.3524],
    ],
  },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gap: 12, inlineSize: "100%" }}>
      <MapView
        aria-label="Tram lines 2 and 12"
        mapStyle={style}
        bounds={mapBoundsOf(lines.flatMap((line) => line.way))}
        boundsPadding={48}
        cooperativeGestures
      >
        {lines.map((line) => (
          <MapRoute
            key={line.name}
            coordinates={line.way}
            width={5}
            // A color of the theme's, by its variable.
            style={{ "--nuv-map-route-color": line.color } as CSSProperties}
          />
        ))}
        <MapControls />
      </MapView>
      {/* A color on a map says nothing to someone who can't see it, so the
          lines are in words as well. */}
      <p style={{ margin: 0, fontSize: "var(--text-sm)" }}>
        Line 2 runs from Vondelpark to Centraal. Line 12 runs from Westerpark to
        Amstel, and crosses line 2 at Leidseplein.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Set them on the route itself with `style` or a `className`, or on anything around the map. They're read when the route is drawn, and again when its props or the page's theme change.

Don't let the color be the only thing that tells two routes apart. Name them in words beside the map.

## CSS variables

The route's variables are in the [MapView](page:map/map-view#css-variables) page's list with the rest of the map's: `--nuv-map-route-color` and `--nuv-map-route-edge`.

```css
.late {
  --nuv-map-route-color: var(--color-danger);
}
```

## The places

`points` is every place, in view or not. Each has an `id` that tells it from the others and a `position`, as longitude and then latitude. Whatever else is on it is yours, and comes back to you.

```tsx
interface Branch extends MapClusterPoint {
  name: string;
  open: boolean;
}
```

The child is a function. It's called for each place in view that stands on its own, with that place, and returns its [MapMarker](page:map/map-view#markers): a pin, a popup, a drawing of your own, as for any marker. It isn't called for the places in a gathering, or for those out of view, so a thousand places don't make a thousand elements.

## CSS variables

A gathering's variables are in the [MapView](page:map/map-view#css-variables) page's list with the rest of the map's: `--nuv-map-cluster-bg`, `--nuv-map-cluster-fg` and `--nuv-map-cluster-size`.

```css
.chargers {
  --nuv-map-cluster-bg: var(--color-danger);
  --nuv-map-cluster-fg: var(--color-danger-foreground);
}
```

A gathering has the class `nuv-map__cluster` and `data-size`, which is `sm` under ten places, `md` under a hundred and `lg` from there.

pages.markdown:
# Flow diagrams

The @nuvui/flow package. Boxes, circles and other shapes joined by arrows, drawn as SVG, in a view that pans and zooms, and edited with a pointer or the keyboard.

Flow diagrams are a package of their own, `@nuvui/flow`. They need no library the core doesn't have, but most apps have no diagrams, and an app without them shouldn't carry one.

| Component | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| [Flow](page:flow/flow) | A diagram of shapes joined by arrows, in a frame that pans and zooms. With `editable`, its nodes are dragged, joined and removed. |

```tsx
import { Flow, type FlowEdge, type FlowNode } from "@nuvui/flow";

const nodes: FlowNode[] = [
  {
    id: "placed",
    label: "Order placed",
    shape: "pill",
    position: { x: 0, y: 0 },
    intent: "primary",
  },
  {
    id: "stock",
    label: "In stock?",
    shape: "diamond",
    position: { x: 8, y: 110 },
  },
  {
    id: "ship",
    label: "Pack and ship",
    position: { x: 260, y: 126 },
  },
  {
    id: "reorder",
    label: "Reorder from the supplier",
    shape: "box",
    position: { x: 0, y: 270 },
    intent: "warning",
  },
  {
    id: "delivered",
    label: "Delivered",
    shape: "pill",
    position: { x: 260, y: 270 },
    intent: "success",
  },
];

const edges: FlowEdge[] = [
  { id: "placed-stock", from: "placed", to: "stock" },
  { id: "stock-ship", from: "stock", to: "ship", label: "yes" },
  { id: "stock-reorder", from: "stock", to: "reorder", label: "no" },
  { id: "reorder-ship", from: "reorder", to: "ship", dashed: true },
  { id: "ship-delivered", from: "ship", to: "delivered" },
];

// Flow is a client component, and this one only hands it data, so it can
// be a server component.
export default function Example() {
  return <Flow aria-label="How an order goes" nodes={nodes} edges={edges} />;
}
```

## Install

```sh
pnpm add @nuvui/flow @nuvui/react
```

`@nuvui/react` is a peer dependency: the diagram is drawn with the core's tokens. Nothing else is installed with it. The panning, the zooming, the dragging and the lines are written in the package.

## Load the styles

Import the package's stylesheet once, after the core's:

```tsx title="app/layout.tsx"
import "@nuvui/react/styles.css";
import "@nuvui/flow/styles.css";
```

Both files open with the same line naming the cascade layers, so the order you import them in doesn't decide which rule wins. The diagram's styles are also on their own, at `@nuvui/flow/css/flow.css`.

## What it keeps and what you keep

The diagram is data: a list of nodes, each with a place and a label, and a list of edges, each from one node to another. You can keep both and hand them in, or hand in the first ones and let the diagram keep them.

```ts
const nodes = [
  { id: "placed", label: "Order placed", shape: "pill", position: { x: 0, y: 0 } },
  { id: "stock", label: "In stock?", shape: "diamond", position: { x: 8, y: 110 } },
];
const edges = [{ id: "placed-stock", from: "placed", to: "stock" }];
```

When a node is moved, joined or removed in the diagram, you're told once, with the lists as they are now and what changed. A drag is told when it ends, not on every frame.

## Large diagrams

Only what's in view is drawn, and a node that moves draws itself and its lines again, and nothing else. From far out, or with too many nodes in view to draw one by one, the diagram is a few paths, one for each color. So the number of elements on the page stays about the same whatever the size of the diagram.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Flow,
  type FlowEdge,
  type FlowIntent,
  type FlowNode,
} from "@nuvui/flow";
import { useMemo } from "react";

const intents: FlowIntent[] = [
  "neutral",
  "primary",
  "success",
  "warning",
  "danger",
];

// Five thousand steps in a grid, each joined to the next in its row and to
// the one under it. Made in the browser, so the page doesn't carry them.
function grid(count: number) {
  const columns = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(count));
  const nodes: FlowNode[] = [];
  const edges: FlowEdge[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    nodes.push({
      id: `n${i}`,
      label: `Step ${i + 1}`,
      position: { x: (i % columns) * 220, y: Math.floor(i / columns) * 120 },
      intent: intents[i % intents.length],
    });
    if (i % columns) {
      edges.push({ id: `h${i}`, from: `n${i - 1}`, to: `n${i}` });
    }
    if (i >= columns) {
      edges.push({
        id: `v${i}`,
        from: `n${i - columns}`,
        to: `n${i}`,
        path: "step",
      });
    }
  }
  return { nodes, edges };
}

export default function Example() {
  const { nodes, edges } = useMemo(() => grid(5000), []);
  return (
    <Flow
      aria-label="Five thousand steps"
      defaultNodes={nodes}
      defaultEdges={edges}
      serverLimit={0}
    />
  );
}
```

## What it leaves out

- **Laying a diagram out by itself.** Every node has a position from you. A library such as dagre or ELK can work positions out, and you hand them in.
- **Anything inside a node but its words.** A node is a shape with a label, drawn in SVG. A button or a field in a node isn't possible.
- **Groups,** a box around some nodes that moves them together.
- **A minimap,** the whole diagram small in a corner.
- **Undo.** The lists are yours, and so is keeping their history.

sections.markdown:
## What it keeps and what you keep

The diagram is data: a list of nodes, each with a place and a label, and a list of edges, each from one node to another. You can keep both and hand them in, or hand in the first ones and let the diagram keep them.

```ts
const nodes = [
  { id: "placed", label: "Order placed", shape: "pill", position: { x: 0, y: 0 } },
  { id: "stock", label: "In stock?", shape: "diamond", position: { x: 8, y: 110 } },
];
const edges = [{ id: "placed-stock", from: "placed", to: "stock" }];
```

When a node is moved, joined or removed in the diagram, you're told once, with the lists as they are now and what changed. A drag is told when it ends, not on every frame.

## Large diagrams

Only what's in view is drawn, and a node that moves draws itself and its lines again, and nothing else. From far out, or with too many nodes in view to draw one by one, the diagram is a few paths, one for each color. So the number of elements on the page stays about the same whatever the size of the diagram.

```tsx
"use client";

import {
  Flow,
  type FlowEdge,
  type FlowIntent,
  type FlowNode,
} from "@nuvui/flow";
import { useMemo } from "react";

const intents: FlowIntent[] = [
  "neutral",
  "primary",
  "success",
  "warning",
  "danger",
];

// Five thousand steps in a grid, each joined to the next in its row and to
// the one under it. Made in the browser, so the page doesn't carry them.
function grid(count: number) {
  const columns = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(count));
  const nodes: FlowNode[] = [];
  const edges: FlowEdge[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    nodes.push({
      id: `n${i}`,
      label: `Step ${i + 1}`,
      position: { x: (i % columns) * 220, y: Math.floor(i / columns) * 120 },
      intent: intents[i % intents.length],
    });
    if (i % columns) {
      edges.push({ id: `h${i}`, from: `n${i - 1}`, to: `n${i}` });
    }
    if (i >= columns) {
      edges.push({
        id: `v${i}`,
        from: `n${i - columns}`,
        to: `n${i}`,
        path: "step",
      });
    }
  }
  return { nodes, edges };
}

export default function Example() {
  const { nodes, edges } = useMemo(() => grid(5000), []);
  return (
    <Flow
      aria-label="Five thousand steps"
      defaultNodes={nodes}
      defaultEdges={edges}
      serverLimit={0}
    />
  );
}
```

## Nodes

A node has an `id`, a `label` and a `position`: its top left corner, in the diagram's own units. The diagram is fitted into its frame, so the units are whatever you like.

`shape` is one of five, each with a meaning a screen reader hears after the label:

| Shape | Read as | Default size |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `box` | step | 160 by 56 |
| `rounded`, the default | step | 160 by 56 |
| `circle` | event | 88 by 88 |
| `diamond` | decision | 144 by 88 |
| `pill` | start or end | 160 by 48 |

`size` changes one node's size. `intent` draws its outline in a color: `neutral`, `primary`, `success`, `warning` or `danger`. `description` is read after its name and shown in the list.

```tsx
import { Flow, type FlowNode } from "@nuvui/flow";

const nodes: FlowNode[] = [
  { id: "box", label: "box", shape: "box", position: { x: 0, y: 0 } },
  {
    id: "rounded",
    label: "rounded",
    shape: "rounded",
    position: { x: 200, y: 0 },
    intent: "primary",
  },
  {
    id: "circle",
    label: "circle",
    shape: "circle",
    position: { x: 400, y: -16 },
    intent: "success",
  },
  {
    id: "diamond",
    label: "diamond",
    shape: "diamond",
    position: { x: 528, y: -16 },
    intent: "warning",
  },
  {
    id: "pill",
    label: "pill",
    shape: "pill",
    position: { x: 712, y: 4 },
    intent: "danger",
  },
];

export default function Example() {
  return (
    <Flow
      aria-label="The five shapes"
      nodes={nodes}
      background="none"
      controls={false}
      className="[--nuv-flow-height:12rem]"
    />
  );
}
```

The label wraps inside the shape, and after as many lines as the shape has room for it ends with an ellipsis. The whole label is still its name. A circle's and a diamond's words sit in the box that fits inside them, so they're narrower than the shape.

## Edges

An edge goes `from` one node `to` another, or to the same node, which draws a loop. `path` says how the line goes:

- `curved`, the default, and `step`, at right angles, join the sides of the two nodes that face each other.
- `straight` runs from one outline to the other, toward the middles.

`edgePath` on the diagram changes the default for every edge that doesn't say.

`arrow` puts an arrowhead at the `end`, the default, the `start`, `both` or `none`. `label` writes words on the line, such as "yes" after a decision. `dashed` draws it dashed, and `animated` draws it dashed and moving toward its end, which stops when less motion is asked for. `intent` colors it as a node's.

```tsx
import { Flow, type FlowEdge, type FlowNode } from "@nuvui/flow";

const box = (id: string, x: number, y: number): FlowNode => ({
  id,
  label: id,
  shape: "box",
  position: { x, y },
  size: { width: 120, height: 48 },
});

const nodes: FlowNode[] = [
  box("Request", 0, 0),
  box("Straight", 300, 0),
  box("Curved", 300, 110),
  box("Step", 300, 220),
  box("Both ways", 0, 220),
];

const edges: FlowEdge[] = [
  { id: "straight", from: "Request", to: "Straight", path: "straight" },
  {
    id: "curved",
    from: "Request",
    to: "Curved",
    path: "curved",
    label: "dashed",
    dashed: true,
  },
  {
    id: "step",
    from: "Request",
    to: "Step",
    path: "step",
    label: "running",
    animated: true,
    intent: "primary",
  },
  {
    id: "both",
    from: "Step",
    to: "Both ways",
    arrow: "both",
    intent: "danger",
  },
];

export default function Example() {
  return <Flow aria-label="Kinds of edges" nodes={nodes} edges={edges} />;
}
```

## Editing

With `editable`, the diagram can be changed:

- **Moving.** Drag a node, or several selected ones. With the keyboard, hold Shift and press an arrow key. `snap` puts moved nodes on a grid of that step, and the arrow keys move by it, or by 10 without one.
- **Joining.** Drag from one of the handles on a node's sides to another node. Handles show on the node under the pointer and on a node that's selected alone. With the keyboard, press L on one node, move to another and press L again.
- **Removing.** Select nodes or edges and press Delete. A node takes its edges with it.

A node with `locked` can be selected but not moved, joined or removed.

```tsx
"use client";

import { Flow, type FlowEdge, type FlowNode } from "@nuvui/flow";
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";
import { useState } from "react";

const start: FlowNode[] = [
  {
    id: "lead",
    label: "New lead",
    shape: "pill",
    position: { x: 0, y: 40 },
    intent: "primary",
  },
  { id: "qualify", label: "Qualify", position: { x: 220, y: 36 } },
  {
    id: "won",
    label: "Won",
    shape: "pill",
    position: { x: 440, y: 40 },
    intent: "success",
  },
];

const links: FlowEdge[] = [
  { id: "lead-qualify", from: "lead", to: "qualify" },
  { id: "qualify-won", from: "qualify", to: "won" },
];

export default function Example() {
  const [nodes, setNodes] = useState(start);
  const [edges, setEdges] = useState(links);
  const [last, setLast] = useState("Nothing yet.");

  const add = () => {
    const count = nodes.length + 1;
    setNodes([
      ...nodes,
      {
        id: `step-${count}`,
        label: `Step ${count}`,
        position: { x: 220, y: 140 + (count - 3) * 80 },
      },
    ]);
  };

  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <Flow
        aria-label="Sales steps"
        editable
        snap={20}
        nodes={nodes}
        edges={edges}
        onNodesChange={(next, change) => {
          setNodes(next);
          setLast(`Nodes: ${change.type} ${change.ids.join(", ")}`);
        }}
        onEdgesChange={(next, change) => {
          setEdges(next);
          setLast(`Edges: ${change.type} ${change.ids.join(", ")}`);
        }}
      />
      <div className="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <Button intent="secondary" onClick={add}>
          Add a step
        </Button>
        <p className="text-sm text-muted-foreground" aria-live="polite">
          {last}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Who keeps the diagram

Pass `nodes` and `edges`, and the diagram draws them and tells you of changes. Pass `defaultNodes` and `defaultEdges` instead, and it keeps them itself and still tells you.

`onNodesChange` is called with the nodes as they are after a change, and the change: `{ type: "move", ids }` or `{ type: "remove", ids }`. A drag is reported once, when it ends, and keys once they rest, so a diagram with thousands of nodes isn't copied on every frame. `onNodeDrag` is called on every frame of a drag for an app that wants that. The nodes that didn't change are the same objects as before.

`onEdgesChange` is the same for edges, with `{ type: "add", ids }` or `{ type: "remove", ids }`.

When you keep `nodes`, a move you don't take is undone: the node goes back to where your nodes say. That's how a step is kept in a lane, or saving that fails is put right.

### Which links may be made

By default any two different nodes may be joined, once each way. `canConnect` decides instead: it's called with the two ids, and a link it refuses isn't made. `createEdge` makes the new edge, which by default is `{ id: "from->to", from, to }`.

## Use an icon

```tsx
import { Button } from "@nuvui/react";
import { ArrowRightIcon } from "@nuvui/icons";

export function Next() {
  return (
    <Button>
      Next <ArrowRightIcon size={16} />
    </Button>
  );
}
```

Every icon's name is its name in the set, written as a component, with `Icon` at the end: `arrow-right` is `ArrowRightIcon`, and `map` is `MapIcon`. That's its only name. A bare `Map` or `Image` would hide JavaScript's own in the file that imports it.

## In a right-to-left layout

An icon is drawn the same way in either direction. One that points the way of reading, such as an arrow for "next", has to be turned around on a right-to-left page. A clock or a check mark doesn't. Whether an arrow points the way of reading depends on what it means where it's used, so that's left to you, with a selector on the `dir` attribute:

```css
[dir="rtl"] .next-icon {
  transform: scaleX(-1);
}
```

[Right-to-left](page:right-to-left) has the rest, and the SCSS mixin that writes that selector.

## What gets bundled

Each icon is a module of its own, and the package says it has no side effects, so a bundler leaves out every icon a page doesn't import. One icon is under half a kilobyte, compressed.

Without that pruning, in a development server, importing from the package loads all 317. Next.js prunes during development too for packages listed in `experimental.optimizePackageImports`:

```js title="next.config.mjs"
export default {
  experimental: {
    optimizePackageImports: ["@nuvui/icons"],
  },
};
```

## An icon of your own

`createIcon` makes a component that's drawn and behaves the way the set's own are, from shapes on the same 24 unit grid:

```tsx
import { createIcon } from "@nuvui/icons";

export const FrameIcon = createIcon("FrameIcon", "standard", [
  ["rect", { x: "4", y: "4", width: "16", height: "16", rx: "2" }],
]);
```

The first argument names it in React's developer tools. The second is the style whose ends and corners it takes.

